Dataspatium.assets.register("docs/en/kom-igang.md","text","# Getting started with Dataspatium\n\nThis guide takes you from download to your first export. It assumes nothing more than a modern browser.\n\n## 1. Download and unpack\n\nFetch the latest release from <https://github.com/marwiss/Dataspatium/releases/latest>: the ZIP file\n`dataspatium-<version>.zip` under *Assets*. Unpack it into a folder of your choice, for example\n`Documents\\Dataspatium`. The folder contains `index.html`, the folders `src`, `vendor` and `docs` and more. Do not\nmove or rename files inside the folder; the whole folder, however, can be moved freely, even to a USB stick.\n\n## 2. Open the app\n\nDouble-click `index.html`. The browser opens the app from your computer (the address starts with `file:///`).\nIf double-clicking does not work, right-click and choose *Open with* and your browser, or drag the file into an\nopen browser window.\n\nThe first time, the app asks which language you want: Swedish, Danish, Norwegian, Finnish, Icelandic or English.\nThe choice is stored in the browser and can be changed at any time with the language button (the icon with\nletters) at the top right, or through the command palette. Fonts and icons are loaded from the folder; that takes\na second. Nothing is fetched from the internet.\n\n### The beginner's course\n\nAfter the language choice, the **beginner's course** starts: an interactive walkthrough in a panel at the bottom\nright. It takes about ten minutes and follows the example *Population per municipality 2024 (Sweden)* through all eight\nmodes: get data, look at the dataset, search, calculate a column, query with SQL, calculate with R, make a chart,\nexport and share.\nEach step explains a concept and gives a task. A frame shows where to click, and the step is ticked off when you have\ndone it. *Do it for me* performs the step for you if you would rather watch how it is done.\n\nThe course can be minimised and closed, and it can be reopened at any time from the help (the question mark at the\ntop right) or from the command palette (Ctrl+K, *Open the beginner's course*). It continues where you left off.\n\n## 3. The modes\n\nAt the top there are eight modes. The panel on the left shows objects (datasets and sources), the middle is the\nwork area, the panel on the right shows properties and statistics, and at the bottom is the status bar with the save\nstate, storage, the selected dataset, the R engine and the buttons that collapse the panels and open the log. Panel\nwidths are changed by dragging the edge towards the work area. Alt+1 to Alt+8 switch mode from the keyboard.\n\n| Mode | What you do there |\n|---|---|\n| Import | Import files, paste tables and fetch from open APIs in Sweden and the other Nordic countries, such as SCB, Kolada, Eurostat, the National Board of Health and Welfare, SMHI, Statistics Denmark, Statistics Norway, Statistics Finland and Statistics Iceland ([api-konfiguration.md](api-konfiguration.md)) |\n| Manage | See your datasets in folders with tags, rename, delete, save versions, run batch actions on several datasets, back up and restore the workspace |\n| Search | Search every column of one or all datasets, narrow with facets and save searches as lists ([soka.md](soka.md)) |\n| Process | Edit cells, sort and filter, clean and type, calculate columns, pivot, join datasets; all with undo, versions and recipes ([bearbeta.md](bearbeta.md)) |\n| Analyse | R console with ggplot2 and the tidyverse, saved scripts and chart export; Python console with pandas and matplotlib; SQL console against the selected dataset; regular expressions; a notebook that becomes a report |\n| Visualise | Build charts with Vega-Lite, D3 templates or R scripts, save, export and create them in batch ([visualisera.md](visualisera.md)) |\n| Export | Download data as CSV, Excel, JSON or SQLite, saved charts as SVG, PNG or PDF and documents from templates as HTML, Word, PDF, Markdown or LaTeX ([dokumentmallar.md](dokumentmallar.md)) |\n| Share | Bundle datasets with charts and templates for colleagues, send them directly to phones and other computers with a QR code ([mobil.md](mobil.md)), push the workspace to GitHub, share through the computer's share menu and publish dashboards as HTML ([dela.md](dela.md)) |\n\n## 4. Import a file\n\n1. Go to *Import* and drop a file on the dashed surface (or click and choose a file). Supported file formats:\n   CSV, TSV and other delimited text files, Excel (xlsx, xls, xlsm, xlsb), ODS, JSON, NDJSON, JSON-stat,\n   GeoJSON, XML, SQLite, GeoPackage, shapefile (ZIP with shp and dbf), fixed-width text and statistical files\n   from SPSS (sav), Stata (dta), SAS (sas7bdat, xpt), SCB's PX format, Parquet and R (rds, RData). Files can also\n   be fetched straight from a web address under *From web address*. The statistical formats are read by R and\n   imported in one step without a preview; the first time this takes a few seconds while R starts. See\n   [format.md](format.md).\n2. Check the settings. Dataspatium guesses the character encoding (UTF-8 or Windows-1252), the delimiter,\n   whether the first row holds headers and whether the decimal separator is a comma or a point. The preview shows\n   the first 100 rows.\n3. Check the columns. Each column gets a type: text, integer, decimal number, date, date and time or yes/no. Codes\n   with leading zeros (municipality codes, postcodes) are kept as text. Change names or types if needed and\n   untick columns you do not want.\n4. Give the dataset a name and click *Import*. You can then open it directly in *Process*.\n\nTip: you can also paste a table from Excel or a web page through *Paste* in the panel on the left.\n\n## 5. Browse and review\n\n*Process* shows the table. Click a column header to sort (ascending, descending, unsorted) and to see the column's\nstatistics on the right: count, missing, distinct, minimum, maximum, mean, sum and most common values. Type in the\nfilter field to show only rows containing the text. Large tables are rendered row by row as you scroll, so even\nhundreds of thousands of rows can be browsed.\n\n## 6. Export\n\nIn *Export* you choose format and file name and click *Export and download*.\n\n- CSV with the profile *Adapted for Excel in Sweden* uses semicolons, decimal commas and UTF-8 with BOM so that\n  Excel in the Nordic countries opens the file correctly straight away. The *Standard* profile gives commas and\n  decimal points.\n- Excel (xlsx) and ODS keep numbers, dates and yes/no as real types.\n- JSON gives a list of objects, XML a file with one element per row.\n- SQLite gives the dataset's own file, which can also be read in R, DB Browser for SQLite and many other tools.\n- Parquet, SPSS (sav), Stata (dta) and RDS are written by R and start the R session when needed.\n\nAll formats can be imported again into Dataspatium.\n\n## 7. Back up\n\nThe datasets live in the browser's database on this computer. They disappear if you clear site data, switch\nbrowser profile or uninstall the browser. Go to *Manage*, choose *Workspace* and click *Back up the workspace*.\nYou get a ZIP file with all datasets. Restore by dropping the ZIP file on the surface *Restore from ZIP*.\n\nIn Edge and Chrome you can also choose a *portable folder*: the workspace is saved as ordinary files (one SQLite\nfile per dataset plus charts and templates) in a folder you choose, for example on a USB stick or in a\nsynchronised folder. Switch on *Save automatically on changes* and the folder is kept up to date. On another\ncomputer you choose the same folder and click *Load from the folder*. After a browser restart it asks once for\npermission to use the folder again; click *Reconnect*.\n\nIf you want to send a selection to a colleague, go to the *Share* mode and the *Bundle* tab: choose datasets and\nwhether charts, dashboards, templates, notebooks and scripts should come along. The colleague opens the bundle under\n*Receive* in the same mode; whatever already exists in their workspace is skipped.\n\n## Common questions\n\n**The page is empty or only shows \"Dataspatium is starting\".** Check that the whole folder was unpacked (not\njust `index.html`) and that you are using a current version of Edge, Chrome or Firefox. Open the browser console\n(F12) and look for red errors; please report them as an issue.\n\n**My å, ä and ö became strange characters.** Change the character encoding in the import step *Settings*, usually\nbetween UTF-8 and Windows-1252.\n\n**Numbers became text.** The column contained some value that could not be read as a number, or numbers with\nleading zeros. Change the type in the *Columns* step to force an interpretation; values that cannot be\ninterpreted become empty.\n\n**Can I share data with a colleague?** Yes, export the dataset (SQLite or CSV) or take a backup of the workspace\nand send the ZIP file. The colleague imports or restores in their Dataspatium.\n\n**Why no server or cloud service?** So that your data stays with you and so that the app works without\ninstallation, accounts or internet.\n\nMore questions and answers are in [faq.md](faq.md). When you want to move on from the first steps,\n[anvandarguide.md](anvandarguide.md) describes the whole workflow from file to report.\n");
Dataspatium.assets.register("docs/en/anvandarguide.md","text","# User guide\n\nThis guide describes how you work in Dataspatium from the first file to the finished report. It assumes that you\nhave opened the app as described in [kom-igang.md](kom-igang.md). Details about each mode are in\n[lagen.md](lagen.md), about file formats in [format.md](format.md), about APIs in\n[api-konfiguration.md](api-konfiguration.md), about processing in [bearbeta.md](bearbeta.md), about R in\n[r-guide.md](r-guide.md), about Python in [python.md](python.md), about charts in [visualisera.md](visualisera.md) and about documents in\n[dokumentmallar.md](dokumentmallar.md). Common questions are answered in [faq.md](faq.md).\n\n## The workspace\n\nThe screen looks the same in every mode. At the top you switch mode (Import, Manage, Search, Process, Analyse,\nVisualise, Export, Share; Alt+1 to Alt+8 work too) and reach the command palette (the magnifying glass or Ctrl+K),\nthe language button, the theme (light or dark) and the help button with the version number and *Check for updates*.\nThe command palette is the fastest way to most things: type a few letters to switch mode, open a dataset, a chart\nor a dashboard, search in data or run a processing action on the selected dataset. On the left are the objects of\nthe mode (datasets, sources, charts, templates), in the middle the work area and on the right the inspector with\nproperties and statistics. At the bottom is the status bar: save state, storage, the selected dataset, the R engine,\ninternet requests in progress and the buttons that collapse the panels and open the log, where every action, warning\nand error is written; that is what you read when something does not turn out as you intended. Panel widths are\nchanged by dragging the edge towards the work area, and in narrow windows the panels open as sheets over the work\narea (see [lagen.md](lagen.md)).\n\nEverything you create is saved automatically in the browser's database on your computer. Nothing is sent anywhere\nunless you yourself fetch from an API, check the version, push to GitHub or share. The first time the app needs the\ninternet for a purpose it asks for permission, and the answer can be remembered; under *Manage → Workspace → Internet\naccess* you see what is allowed and which requests have been made. Parts of the app that do not ship in the folder\n(some components and R packages) are fetched the same way when needed and then stored locally.\n\n## The beginner's course\n\nThe beginner's course is a good way to get to know the app. It starts after the language choice the first time the\napp is opened, and otherwise from the help (the question mark at the top right) or with the command *Open the\nbeginner's course* in the command palette. The course is shown in a panel at the bottom right while you work in the\nreal app, and it follows the example *Population per municipality 2024 (Sweden)* in nine steps:\n\n1. Welcome: what the app does and how the screen is arranged.\n2. Import: import the example data, and what a dataset is.\n3. Manage: name and short name, source, columns, versions and backups.\n4. Process: the table, column profiles and a calculated column that becomes a step in the recipe.\n5. Analyse with SQL: the population per county, and how short names combine datasets.\n6. Analyse with R: `dm_read()`, a summary and a histogram with ggplot2.\n7. Visualise: a horizontal bar chart of the population per county.\n8. Export: data, charts and documents.\n9. Done: where to go next.\n\nEach step explains a concept and gives a task. A frame marks the button to click, and the step is ticked off when the\napp sees that the task is done. *Do it for me* (or *Show me*) performs the step, so you can follow along without\ntyping anything. The course can be minimised with the arrow and closed with the cross; next time it continues where\nyou left off. It creates the example dataset, a column in it and a chart, which you delete as usual when you no longer\nneed them.\n\n## A workflow from file to report\n\n### 1. Import data (Import)\n\nDrop a file on the surface, paste a table or fetch a file from a web address (the server must allow CORS; a shared\nDataspatium configuration is added directly). Check the preview: character encoding, delimiter, header row and\ndecimal separator are guessed but can be changed. In the column step each column gets a type; codes with leading\nzeros (municipality codes, personal identity numbers) are kept as text. Give the dataset a name and import. The\narea below the surface has data that ships with the app: the button for the example data *Population per\nmunicipality 2024 (Sweden)* and the list *Boundaries for maps* with municipality and region boundaries for Sweden,\nDenmark, Norway and Finland.\n\nUnder *Open APIs* there are presets for SCB, the Public Health Agency of Sweden, the National Board of Health and\nWelfare, Kolada, Eurostat, SMHI, air quality data, the Nature Conservation Register, Lantmäteriet's geodata\ncatalogue and Sweden's data portal, as well as Boverket and Lantmäteriet Direkt for those who have their own keys.\nIn addition there are the statistics offices and several other agencies in Denmark, Norway, Finland and Iceland,\nthe self-governing areas and Nordic Statistics; the country buttons above the list choose which countries'\nsources are shown. Browse or search for a table, choose variables, preview and fetch into a new dataset or add to an existing one. A\nfetched dataset remembers its query, so it can be fetched again from Manage when the source has been updated, and\n*Saved fetches* in Import updates such datasets with one click and replays their recipes.\n\nDatabases: Access files are dropped on the surface like other files; databases on the computer (SQL Server, ODBC,\nExcel, SQLite) are reached through *Database through the bridge* and services over HTTP (PostgREST, OData, GraphQL\nand more) through *Add a database source*. In Chrome and Edge a file can be linked and read again from disk. See\n[kallor.md](kallor.md).\n\n### 2. Review and correct (Process)\n\nThe table shows the dataset row by row even when it has hundreds of thousands of rows. Click a header to sort and\nsee the column's statistics on the right: count, missing, distinct, minimum, maximum, mean and most common values.\nFilter with the search field. Double-click a cell to change it.\n\n*Data quality* goes through all columns and points at problems (numbers stored as text, mixed date formats,\nduplicates, encoding errors, deviating categories) with a button that performs the right action. *Rules* let you\nwrite down what must hold (must not be missing, unique, range, list, pattern, length, SQL condition), run the check\nat any time, save failing rows as a dataset of their own and download a report. All actions are also in the\npalette: remove empty rows, trim, case, find and replace with regular expressions, change type, parse dates and\nnumbers, fill missing values, duplicates, cluster similar categories and more. Every action is logged as a recipe\nstep and can be undone. Before sharing, identifiers such as personal identity numbers can be pseudonymised with\nsalted hashes, masking or running numbers; the salt is never stored in the recipe.\n\n### 3. Combine and reshape (Process)\n\nCalculated columns, split and combine columns, filter out rows, rename and reorder, pivot between wide and long\nform, group and aggregate, join two datasets on a key and append datasets with the same columns. Aggregations,\njoins and appends become new datasets, so the origin remains. The group *Geodata* gives centroid and area from\ngeometries, conversion between SWEREF 99 TM and WGS 84 and municipality and county from coordinates with the\nbundled boundaries.\n\nThe recipe (all steps in order) can be shown, replayed on a new version of the data and exported as JSON, R code or\nSQL. Versions are taken at import, before bulk operations and on request; two versions can be compared and an\nolder one restored.\n\n### 4. Analyse with R and SQL (Analyse)\n\nThe R console runs real R (WebR) in the browser. `dm_list()` shows the datasets, `dm_read(\"Name\")` reads one as a\ndata.frame with the right types, `dm_write(df, \"New name\")` sends a data.frame back as a new dataset.\n`theme_dataspatium()` and `dm_palette()` give ggplot2 the app's look. Charts are shown in the panel and exported as\nSVG, PNG or PDF. Packages such as ggplot2, dplyr, tidyr, data.table, haven and readxl ship with the app and are\ninstalled in the session the first time they are used. Scripts can be saved and run again. The first start takes a\nfew seconds.\n\nThe SQL tab queries the dataset's table `data` directly with SQLite. Other datasets are reached by their short\nnames (shown below the name in the object panel), so one query can join several datasets, for example\n`select ... from befolkning_2024 b join kommungranser k on k.kommunkod = b.kommunkod`. SELECT queries show the\nresult (up to 5,000 rows) and can be saved as a new dataset; UPDATE, DELETE and ALTER are logged as recipe steps.\nThe button *Insert dm_read()* and the R examples in the inspector use the short name too.\n\nThe *Notebook* tab gathers text (Markdown), R and SQL in cells that run in order and save their output. A finished\nnotebook is exported as HTML, Word, PDF, Markdown or LaTeX, with or without the code. See\n[anteckningsbok.md](anteckningsbok.md).\n\nThe *Regex* tab tries a regular expression on a column as you type: the most common values are shown with the\nmatches marked and the matching rows are counted. The same pattern can then filter rows, extract groups into new\ncolumns, replace, split, flag or count matches, or become a quality rule. *Pattern profile* shows the shapes of the\nvalues (for example `9999-99-99` and `99/99/9999` for dates), and the library on the left has ready-made patterns\nfor personal identity numbers, postcodes and dates, among others. See [regex.md](regex.md).\n\n### 5. Visualise (Visualise)\n\nThe chart builder creates bar, line, area, point, pie and other charts from a dataset: choose columns for axes,\ncolour and facets, aggregation, sorting and titles. D3 templates give special charts (lollipop, slope chart,\npopulation pyramid) and R charts can be saved with their code. All engines use the same colours and fonts. Saved\ncharts are gathered in the gallery and exported as SVG, PNG, PDF or PowerPoint, one at a time or one per group (for\nexample per municipality) to a ZIP or a presentation. Maps are drawn with the chart type *Map* from a dataset with\ngeometry (the bundled municipality or county boundaries, or your own from GeoJSON, shapefile and GeoPackage)\njoined to values per municipality code, and with *Point map* from longitude and latitude with the boundaries as\nbackground. A dashboard gathers several saved charts in a grid with shared filters, for example county or year,\nand is exported as standalone HTML or PowerPoint.\n\n### 6. Documents (Export, the Documents tab)\n\nDocument templates are written in Markdown with placeholders: `{{datamangd}}`, `{{antal_rader}}`,\n`{{#tabell}}…{{/tabell}}` for rows, `{{diagram \"Name\"}}` for a saved chart, `{{sidbrytning}}` and key figures\nsuch as `{{summor.kolumn}}`. Report, codebook and group summary are built in. Generate as HTML, Word, PDF, Markdown\nor LaTeX, one at a time or one document per group.\n\n### 7. Export data (Export, the Data tab)\n\nCSV (adapted for Excel in Sweden or standard), Excel, ODS, JSON, GeoJSON (for datasets with geometry), XML, SQLite\nand, via R: Parquet, SPSS, Stata and RDS. SQLite and Parquet keep the types exactly; all formats can be imported\nagain.\n\n## Finding things in data (Search)\n\nSearch finds rows without code. Type one or more words, choose all datasets or only the selected one and press\n*Search*. Hits are shown per dataset; the facets (the most common values in columns with few distinct values) narrow\nthe search with one click, and a row in the table shows the whole record in the inspector. The search can be saved as\na list, become a new dataset or be opened in Process with the dataset selected. The *Query* tab builds a query over\none or several datasets with columns, calculations, conditions and sorting, without code, and shows the SQL it\ncreates; saved lists narrow the table in Process and the rows of charts. See [soka.md](soka.md).\n\n## Keeping order (Manage)\n\nEvery dataset has an overview (source, size, description, folder and tags), a column list with types, labels,\ndescriptions and units (set under Process with *Label, description and unit*), comments, a readable history of\neverything done and versions. Rename and change the short name, organise, delete or re-fetch from API (update with\nthe recipe, or add rows). Under *Workspace* you see how much storage is used, request persistent storage, take a\nbackup as ZIP and restore. The app reminds you at start when more than the chosen number of days have passed since\nthe last backup (30 days by default; changed or switched off in the same card). In Edge and Chrome the workspace can\nalso be mirrored to a portable folder on disk, automatically on every change.\n\nThe object panel in Manage is a tree. *Organise* gives a dataset a folder (with subfolders, such as `Economy/2026`)\nand tags; folders can be collapsed, and a dataset can be dragged to another folder. The filter field searches name,\nshort name, tag and folder, and the list can be sorted by changed, name or rows. The checkboxes select several\ndatasets (Shift selects a range), and the button *Actions (n)* does the same thing with all of them at once:\norganise, bundle, export as ZIP with CSV files, re-fetch from API, run a recipe or delete. Folder and tags are stored\nin the dataset's file and travel in bundles, backups and GitHub; in R, `dm_list()` shows the folder and\n`dm_read(\"Folder/Name\")` reads a dataset in a folder.\n\n## Sharing with others\n\nEverything meant for other people is in the *Share* mode (see [dela.md](dela.md)):\n\n- A selection: *Bundle* gives a ZIP with chosen datasets, charts, dashboards, templates, notebooks and scripts, a\n  message and a change log: what was done with each dataset since it was last shared, and the comments that are not\n  resolved. The recipient opens it under *Receive*, sees the message and the changes and chooses, for datasets that\n  already exist, whether to replace them with the bundle's version (their own is kept under *Versions*); the rest is\n  skipped. Under Manage, *Actions* on several selected datasets does the same.\n- Comments: every dataset has the tab *Comments* in Manage, and in Process you comment on a column from the inspector.\n  The comments live in the dataset's file and travel in bundles, backups, the portable folder and GitHub. You give the\n  name shown yourself; it is stored only in the browser.\n- The whole workspace: the backup (ZIP) is restored under Manage on another computer.\n- Between computers through GitHub: the *GitHub* tab pushes the workspace to a GitHub repository (preferably private)\n  and fetches it on another computer. This needs a fine-grained personal access token that may only read and write the\n  contents of that repository. The token is used for the session or stored encrypted, and nothing is sent until you\n  press *Push to GitHub* or *Fetch from GitHub*. Only changed files are sent, in one commit with your message; when\n  fetching you choose, as for bundles, whether existing datasets are replaced.\n- Directly to a phone or another computer on the same network: the *Devices* tab shows a QR code that the phone\n  scans; Dataspatium Mobile receives, shows and forwards (see [mobil.md](mobil.md)). Both devices show the same\n  verification code, and after pairing one scan is enough.\n- Through the computer's or phone's share menu: the *Share menu* tab sends a dataset as CSV, Excel, ODS or JSON, or\n  all datasets as a bundle, to e-mail, Teams and other apps (Edge and Chrome; Firefox saves the file).\n- Review first: when datasets are bundled or sent, the app looks for identity numbers, e-mail and phone numbers and\n  lets you pseudonymise or drop the columns in the copy.\n- As a finished web page: the *Publish* tab turns a dashboard or a document into a standalone HTML file.\n- One dataset: export as SQLite (everything comes along) or Parquet under Export.\n- API configurations (`.api.json`), charts and document templates (JSON) and recipes (JSON, R or SQL) are exported\n  and loaded as files. Keys never come along.\n\n## Help in the app\n\nThe whole handbook is in the app: the **?** button at the top right, the **F1** key or the *Help* command in the\ncommand palette opens it at the chapter of the mode you are in. The contents are on the left, above them a search\nfield that searches every section, and links between the documents are followed inside the handbook. Dialogs with a\n**?** button open the right section directly. The handbook follows the app's language and needs no network; at the\nbottom are the version, *Check for updates* and the link to the documentation on GitHub.\n\n## Keyboard\n\nAlt+1 to Alt+8 switch mode, Ctrl+K opens the command palette and F1 opens the handbook. The edge between a panel and the work area can\nreceive focus with Tab and be moved with the arrow keys (Shift for larger steps, Home and End for the smallest and\nlargest width). The first Tab press on the page shows the link\n*Skip to content*, which moves focus past the mode switcher and the object panel. Everything is reachable with\nTab and Enter; dialogs close with Esc. In the table the arrow keys move between cells and Enter edits. The app\nfollows the system setting for reduced motion.\n\nTabs are switched with the left and right arrow keys, Home and End. Every button, field and code editor has a name\nthat screen readers announce, dialogs are announced with their title and the page has landmarks for the mode\nswitcher, the object panel, the work area and the inspector. Text and field borders have WCAG AA contrast in both the\nlight and the dark theme. Charts from the chart builder are described for screen readers by Vega; for R charts and\nD3 templates the figures are in the table in Process.\n\n## Language\n\nThe interface is available in Swedish, Danish, Norwegian (Bokmål), Finnish, Icelandic and English. The first time\nthe app is opened you choose a language; the language button at the top right (or the *Language* command in the\npalette) changes it later. The choice is stored in the browser, so each computer and browser profile has its own.\nNumbers in the interface follow the language's conventions; the data itself is never changed. R's messages follow\nthe same choice, while template placeholders such as `{{datamangd}}` and the table keywords `kolumner`, `sortera`,\n`topp`, `filter` and `rubrik` are part of the template syntax and stay the same in every language. The\ndocumentation exists in all six languages under `docs/` (Swedish in `docs/`, the others in `docs/<language>/`).\n\n## When something goes wrong\n\nRead the log at the bottom: errors you can fix are described in your language with suggestions. If R hangs, use\n*Restart R*; the datasets are not affected. Take a backup before clearing browser data. Bugs are reported as issues\non GitHub, see [CONTRIBUTING.md](../../CONTRIBUTING.md).\n");
Dataspatium.assets.register("docs/en/lagen.md","text","# The eight modes\n\nDataspatium is divided into eight modes that follow the flow of the work, in the spirit of statistics production's\ncollect, process, analyse and disseminate: Import, Manage, Search, Process, Analyse, Visualise, Export, Share. All\nmodes share the same skeleton: the mode switcher at the top, the object panel on the left, the work area in the\nmiddle, the inspector on the right and the status bar at the bottom, with the log folded in above it. The panels\nnever change places. Alt+1 to Alt+8 switch mode. An empty workspace opens in Import, a workspace with datasets in\nManage. The buttons at the top right open the command palette, change language, change theme (light, dark, follow\nthe system) and open the help.\n\n**The status bar** shows the save state, how much storage the workspace uses, the selected dataset (name, rows and\ncolumns; a click opens it in Manage), the state of the R engine (off, starting, ready, busy; a click starts it),\ninternet requests in progress (a click shows the log of requests), the buttons that collapse and expand the object\npanel, the inspector and the log, and the number of lines in the log.\n\n**Panel widths** are changed by dragging the edge between a panel and the work area; a double-click resets, and with\nthe keyboard the arrow keys move the edge (Shift for larger steps, Home and End for the smallest and largest width).\nThe widths are remembered. In narrower windows (below 1,100 px) the mode switcher shows only icons, with text on\nthe active mode, and the inspector opens as a sheet over the work area; below 760 px the object panel does the same.\nA sheet closes with Esc or a click in the work area.\n\n**Tooltips** appear for all buttons when the pointer rests on them or when they receive keyboard focus; on a touch\nscreen by holding a finger on the button.\n\nThe command palette (Ctrl+K or the magnifying glass at the top) reaches everything from one search field: switch\nmode, open a dataset, a chart or a dashboard, run a processing action on the selected dataset, export, open the\nworkspace, search in data, create a bundle, copy a link to here, change theme or check for updates. Links have the\nform `index.html#/<mode>/<short name>` (or a chart name in Visualise, a search text in Search) and work from a\nfolder too. Arrow up and down select, Enter runs\nand Esc closes; the most recently run commands are at the top.\n\n## Import\n\nHere data comes in: files dropped on the surface, pasted text, web addresses and open APIs. File import goes in\nsteps: file, settings with preview, columns with types, done. Formats: CSV and other delimited text, fixed-width\ntext, Excel, ODS, JSON (including JSON-stat and GeoJSON), XML, SQLite, GeoPackage, shapefile and, via R, SPSS,\nStata, SAS, PX, Parquet, RDS and RData (see [format.md](format.md)); the R formats are imported in one step without\npreview. Under Open APIs there are presets for Statistics Sweden (browse the subject tree or search for a table), the\nPublic Health Agency of Sweden, the National Board of Health and Welfare, Kolada, Eurostat, SMHI, air quality data,\nthe nature conservation register, Lantmäteriet's geodata catalogue and Sweden's data portal, as well as Boverket and\nLantmäteriet Direkt for those who have keys, and in addition sources in Denmark, Norway, Finland, Iceland, Greenland,\nthe Faroe Islands and Åland and Nordic Statistics; the country buttons above the list choose which countries'\nsources are shown. Your own configurations are added from file and can be shared. *Saved fetches* lists the datasets\nthat came from an API and updates them with one click, one at a time or all. The first time the app needs to\ncontact the internet for a purpose (APIs, web addresses, GitHub, components) it asks for permission; the answer can\nbe remembered. See [api-konfiguration.md](api-konfiguration.md).\n\nUnder *Databases* there are two ways in: *Database through the bridge* (Access, Excel, SQL Server, SQLite and ODBC\nsources on the computer through a small local script) and *Add a database source* (PostgREST, OData, GraphQL,\nCouchDB, ClickHouse, Elasticsearch, SPARQL, InfluxDB, Google Sheets and Airtable over HTTP). Access files are also\nread directly as files, and in Chrome and Edge a file can be linked so that the dataset is read again from disk.\nSee [kallor.md](kallor.md).\n\n## Manage\n\nThe datasets' home. The object panel is a tree: datasets can live in folders with subfolders (`Economy/2026`) and\nhave tags; a filter field searches name, short name, tag and folder, and the list can be sorted by changed, name or\nrows. Folders collapse and expand, and datasets can be dragged to a folder. The checkboxes select several datasets\n(Shift selects a range) and the button *Actions (n)* opens the batch actions: organise (folder and tags), bundle,\nexport as ZIP with CSV files, re-fetch from API, run a recipe and delete. Folder and tags travel with the dataset in\nits file, in bundles and in backups.\n\nThe overview of a dataset shows metadata, the column list, comments, a history of what has been done with the\ndataset and versions, and here you rename and change the short name (the short name is used in SQL, R and chart\nfiles), organise, delete and re-fetch from API. Under *Workspace* you find how much storage is used, persistent\nstorage, backup to ZIP and restore, in Edge and Chrome a portable folder (the workspace is saved as ordinary files in\na folder you choose, automatically after every change if you like, and can be loaded on another computer),\n*Internet access* (which purposes may contact the internet, with a log of requests) and *Components* (parts of the\napp and R packages that are fetched when needed, with size, source and buttons to fetch in advance or remove).\nBundles and GitHub have moved to the Share mode.\n\n## Search\n\nFind and list without code. The *Search* tab searches all columns in one or all datasets; every word must occur on\nthe row, regardless of case. Hits are shown per dataset with counts, facets (the most common values in columns with\nfew distinct values, which narrow the search with one click) and a table where a row opens the whole record in the\ninspector. A search can be saved as a list, become a new dataset or be opened in Process. The *Query* tab builds a\nquery over one or several datasets (columns, calculations, conditions, sorting) without code and shows the SQL it\ncreates; it can be opened in Analyse, saved as a list or become a dataset. The *Lists* tab collects saved searches\nand queries per dataset and runs them again on today's data; lists also narrow the table in Process and the rows of\ncharts. See [soka.md](soka.md).\n\n## Process\n\nEverything you do with the table through buttons and dialogs: edit cells directly in the table, sort, filter and\nsee column statistics; actions for cleaning (search and replace with regular expressions, trim, case, encoding\nrepair, replace value, fill missing, duplicates, category cleaning), types (number, date, yes/no), structure\n(calculated columns, split and combine, delete rows, pivot) and new datasets (group and aggregate, join, append).\nThe data quality profile points at what should be corrected, and quality rules (must not be missing, unique, range,\nlist, pattern, length, SQL condition) check the data on request with a report and the failing rows as a dataset of\ntheir own. Everything is logged as a recipe that can be undone, replayed on another dataset and exported as R or\nSQL code. Versions save snapshots to go back to. See [bearbeta.md](bearbeta.md).\n\n## Analyse\n\nCode and statistics. The tools come in a fixed order: R, Python, SQL, Regex and last the Notebook, which joins pieces from\nthe others. The Python console has pandas, matplotlib, seaborn and plotly and is fetched as a component the first time you start it (see\n[python.md](python.md)). The R console has ggplot2, dplyr, tidyr and other packages,\nsaved scripts, charts with export to SVG, PNG and PDF, and `dm_read()`/`dm_write()` to move data between the app and\nR (see [r-guide.md](r-guide.md)); packages that do not ship with the app are fetched from webR's package repository\nwhen the code needs them, after you have given permission. The SQL console works against the selected dataset\n(`data`), where other datasets are reached by their short names so that a query can join several datasets; queries\nshow results that can be saved as a new dataset, modifying statements are logged as recipe steps (see\n[bearbeta.md](bearbeta.md)). The Regex tab tries regular expressions on a column with the matches highlighted and\nuses them to filter, extract, replace, split, mark, count or create a quality rule, with a pattern profile and a\nlibrary of Nordic patterns (see [regex.md](regex.md)). The notebook mixes text (Markdown), R and SQL in cells that\nrun in order and become a report in HTML, Word, PDF, Markdown or LaTeX (see\n[anteckningsbok.md](anteckningsbok.md)). Later: statistical models.\n\n## Visualise\n\nCharts with three engines and one look: the chart builder (Vega-Lite) with bars, lines, areas, points, histograms,\nheat maps, box plots, pies and maps (choropleth maps from GeoJSON or shapefile data); D3 templates for ranking, slope\ncharts and population pyramids; and R scripts with ggplot2. Saved charts can be exported as SVG, PNG, PDF and\nPowerPoint, shared as viz files, shown in a gallery and created in batch per group. Dashboards gather saved charts\nin a grid with shared filters and are exported as HTML or PowerPoint. See [visualisera.md](visualisera.md).\n\n## Export\n\nFiles to your own computer, in three tabs. **Data**: CSV (Excel-friendly or standard), Excel, ODS, JSON, XML,\nSQLite and, via R, Parquet, SPSS, Stata and RDS. **Charts**: saved charts and dashboards (also all charts as one\nPowerPoint) as SVG, PNG and PDF, singly or in batch per group to ZIP. **Documents**: reports, codebooks and\ncompilations from templates in Markdown with placeholders, as HTML, Word, PDF, Markdown or LaTeX, singly or in batch\nper group (see [dokumentmallar.md](dokumentmallar.md)). What is meant for other people is in Share.\n\n## Share\n\nGive data to others. **Bundle**: a ZIP with chosen datasets, charts, dashboards, templates, notebooks and scripts, a\nmessage and a change log, which the recipient opens under *Receive* and can replace their own versions with.\n**GitHub**: the workspace as a folder in a repository, push and fetch, with an access token stored encrypted.\n**Share menu**: the computer's or phone's own share menu for a file or a bundle (e-mail, Teams, messaging apps).\n**Publish**: dashboards and documents as standalone HTML files. **Devices**: direct transfer to phones and other computers on the same network with a QR code, a\nverification code and pairing, and Dataspatium Mobile on the phone (see [mobil.md](mobil.md)). **Receive**:\nbundles from a file or a web address, with sender and changes visible before anything is taken in. Datasets that\nleave the app are reviewed for personal data first. See [dela.md](dela.md).\n");
Dataspatium.assets.register("docs/en/soka.md","text","# Search\n\nThe Search mode finds and lists without code. It sits between Manage and Process: first you find what you are\nlooking for, then you process it. Three tabs: *Search* (free text with facets), *Query* (a visual query builder over\none or several datasets that shows the SQL it creates) and *Lists* (saved searches and queries).\n\n## Search\n\nType a word or a phrase in the search field, choose whether to search all datasets or only the selected one, and\npress Search (or Enter). All columns are searched, whatever their type, and every word must occur somewhere on the\nrow. Case does not matter, nor for å, ä and ö. Characters that otherwise have a special meaning in patterns (dot,\nparentheses, asterisk) here mean only themselves; for pattern search there is the Regex tab in Analyse.\n\nHits are shown per dataset with counts. Each card has:\n\n- **Facets**: for columns with few distinct values among the hits, the most common values are shown with counts.\n  Click a value to narrow the search to it; the active restrictions are shown as labelled buttons and are removed\n  with a click. Columns where every hit has its own value (names, identifiers) are not shown as facets, since they\n  do not narrow anything.\n- **A table** with the first hundred hits. Click a row to see the whole record in the inspector on the right, with\n  the row number and the button *Open in Process*.\n- **Save as list** saves the search (text and restrictions) for that dataset under the Lists tab.\n- **Save as dataset** turns the hits into a new dataset with the same columns and types.\n- **Open in Process** selects the dataset and switches mode.\n\nThe search runs in SQLite in a worker, so the page does not lock up. In a dataset of a million rows a search takes\nabout a second per word.\n\n## Query\n\nThe Query tab builds a query without you writing SQL, and shows the SQL it creates. The **Datasets** card chooses the\ndataset to query and joins more with *Add join*: pick the dataset, whether only rows with a match in both or all\nrows of the first should be included, and which columns must match (more keys can be added; joined datasets need\nshort names). **Columns** chooses what is shown, as a value or as a calculation (count, distinct count, sum, mean,\nminimum, maximum) with an optional name in the result; when there is a calculation the rows are grouped by the other\ncolumns automatically. **Conditions** are added in groups where all conditions must hold or one is enough (is, is\nnot, greater than, at least, less than, at most, contains, starts with, is one of, is between, is empty, is not\nempty), and **Sorting and count** finish the query. *Run the query* shows the result as a table with the generated\nSQL below; *Open in Analyse* puts the SQL in the SQL console, where it can be changed further. In the SQL the first\ndataset is called `t0` and the joined ones `t1`, `t2` and so on.\n\n*Save as list* saves the query under Lists, where it runs again on today's data, and *Save as dataset* turns the\nresult into a new dataset with the result's columns.\n\n## Lists\n\nA list is a saved search in a dataset: the text, the restrictions and the number of hits the last time it was run.\n*Run* shows today's hits in the Search tab (the data may have changed since the list was saved), *Save as dataset*\nturns the hits into a new dataset and *Delete* removes the list. Lists follow the dataset: if the dataset is\ndeleted, its lists disappear.\n\nLists are used elsewhere too. In Process, *List* in the table's toolbar picks a saved search that narrows the table\n(the badge shows rows of the total), and in the chart builder *List* below the filters picks the rows a chart is\nbuilt on; the choice travels with the chart into dashboards and exports and runs again on today's data. Queries\nsaved from the Query tab are shown with their SQL under Lists and run in Query.\n\n## The command palette\n\nCtrl+K finds objects and commands as before. The command *Search in data* opens Search with the search field in\nfocus.\n\n## Keyboard\n\nEnter in the search field searches. The facet buttons and the table rows are reached with Tab; a row opens in the\ninspector with Enter.\n");
Dataspatium.assets.register("docs/en/bearbeta.md","text","# Process and the SQL console\n\nProcess mode is everything you do with the table through buttons and dialogs: correct cells, clean text, change\ntypes, calculate new columns, pivot, join datasets. Every action is logged as a step in the dataset's recipe, can be\nundone and can be replayed on another dataset. The SQL console in Analyse mode works against the same dataset and\nlogs changes in the same way.\n\n## The table\n\n- Double-click a cell, or select a row and press Enter or F2, to edit. Enter saves, Esc cancels, Tab moves on to the\n  next cell. The value is interpreted according to the column's type: if you type text in a number column you get an\n  error message and the cell keeps its value. Empty text becomes a missing value.\n- The arrow keys move the selection, Page Up/Page Down page through, Home/End jump to the first or last row.\n- Click a column header to sort (ascending, descending, unsorted). The properties panel on the right then shows\n  column statistics (count, missing, distinct, minimum, maximum, mean, sum, most common values) and the actions\n  that suit the column's type. Shift-click shows the statistics without changing the sort.\n- The filter field searches all columns. Editing works in a filtered selection too.\n- *List* in the toolbar (shown when the dataset has saved searches from Search) narrows the table to the list's\n  rows; the badge shows the count of the total. Editing and actions work as usual.\n- The table is virtualised: only the visible rows exist in the browser's DOM, the rest stays in SQLite. A million\n  rows browse as fast as a hundred.\n\n## Actions\n\nThe **Actions** button opens the palette. Each action has a dialog with fields generated from the action's\nparameters, and where possible a preview shows how many rows are affected and examples before and after.\n\n| Group | Action | What it does |\n|---|---|---|\n| Columns | Rename column | New column name; the label follows if it was equal to the name |\n| | Label, description and unit | The label is the display name in table headers and charts; description and unit are shown in the inspector and the codebook, travel with the SQLite file and reach R as the attributes `description` and `unit` |\n| | Value labels | Names for the column's codes, one per line `code = label` (for example `1 = Man`); the values are not changed. The labels are shown in the inspector (also next to the most common values) and the codebook, reach R as `haven::labelled` and are written to .sav and .dta. Empty text removes them |\n| | Delete columns | Removes the chosen columns |\n| | Reorder columns | Move columns up and down |\n| | Change type | Text to integer, decimal number, date, date and time or yes/no. Decimal separator and date format can be given; values that cannot be interpreted become empty and the count is reported |\n| | Copy column | A copy with a new name, for example before the original is changed |\n| | Clean column names | All names become lower-case letters, digits and underscores (`Folkmängd 2024` becomes `folkmangd_2024`), easy to type in SQL and R; the labels keep the original names |\n| Values | Find and replace | Text or regular expression in chosen text columns, with preview |\n| | Trim whitespace | Leading, trailing and (optionally) double spaces |\n| | Change case | UPPER CASE, lower case or Title Case |\n| | Repair character encoding | Fixes mojibake (Ã¶ becomes ö) that arose at import with the wrong encoding |\n| | Replace value | One exact value for another; an empty value can be chosen as from or to |\n| | Recode values | Several values at once from a list with one line per value, `old = new` (for example `01 = Stockholms län`); other values are kept or emptied |\n| | Swap codes for labels | Replaces the column's codes with their value labels, or back again. The labels are kept, so .sav and .dta get the codes back on export; values without a label are left as they are, and back to codes the column becomes numbers if all codes are numbers |\n| | Fill missing values | Constant value, the previous or next row's value, mean, median or most common value |\n| | Round numbers | To a chosen number of decimals: to nearest (a half rounds away from zero), down, up or by dropping the decimals; a negative number rounds to tens (−1), hundreds (−2) and so on |\n| | Pad with leading zeros | To a given length, for example the municipality code 114 to 0114; numbers become text so that the zeros are kept |\n| | Remove characters | Keep only digits (phone and organisation numbers), keep only letters and spaces, remove punctuation and symbols, or remove all spaces |\n| Rows | Delete rows | SQL condition, for example `befolkning < 1000`; choose Keep to keep only the matching rows instead |\n| | Remove duplicates | On chosen columns or the whole row; the first occurrence is kept |\n| | Remove empty rows and columns | Rows where every value is missing and, if you like, columns without any values |\n| | Sort rows | Changes the order of the rows permanently, ascending or descending on one column, so that exports and documents get the same order; empty values go last |\n| | Keep the top rows | The rows with the largest or smallest value, in total or per group (for example the three largest municipalities in each county) |\n| | Random sample | A number of rows or a percentage; the same seed always gives the same sample so that the recipe can be replayed |\n| Structure | New calculated column | SQL expression, for example `round(befolkning / 1000.0, 1)` or `case when andel > 5 then 'stor' else 'liten' end` |\n| | Combine columns | Several columns into one text column with a separator |\n| | Split column | One text column into several at a delimiter |\n| | Extract part of text | A new column with a number of characters counted from the start or the end of the text, from a chosen position (the county code is the first two characters of the municipality code) |\n| | Pivot to long format | Chosen columns become rows with name and value |\n| | Pivot to wide format | The values in a name column become new columns |\n| | SQL statement | Any UPDATE, DELETE or ALTER TABLE against the table `data` |\n| Calculations | Share of total | Each value's share of the total as a percentage or a ratio, in total or per group (the municipality's share of the county's population) |\n| | Rank | Place in the order, largest or smallest first, in total or per group; equal values get the same place (with or without a gap after) or consecutive places |\n| | Running total | The cumulative sum in the order given by another column, in total or per group |\n| | Change from previous | The difference or the percentage change from the previous row in the order given by another column, for example from the previous year per municipality |\n| | Group into classes | Numbers into classes with your own lower limits (`0, 18, 65` gives `0–17`, `18–64` and `65–`), a number of equally wide classes or groups with equally many rows (quantiles) |\n| | Standardise | z-scores (the distance from the mean in standard deviations, sample standard deviation) or the values scaled to 0–1 |\n| | Extract date part | Year, quarter, month, ISO week, weekday (1 = Monday), day, day of year, year and month (`2024-03`) or hour from a date |\n| | Time between dates | Number of whole days, weeks, months or years between two dates, for example age; without an end date it counts to today's date when the step runs |\n| Regular expressions | Filter with a pattern | Keeps or removes rows depending on whether a column matches a regular expression |\n| | Extract with a pattern | New columns from the pattern's groups or from the whole match, for example the year in a text |\n| | Flag matches | A yes/no column that shows whether the value matches |\n| | Count matches | A column with the number of matches in each value |\n| | Split with a pattern | Splits a column into several where the pattern matches; the last part gets the rest of the text |\n| New datasets | Group and aggregate | Sum, mean, minimum, maximum, count, distinct count or median per group, into a new dataset |\n| | Join datasets | Left or inner join with another dataset on a key |\n| | Append datasets | Stacks rows from several datasets; columns are matched by name |\n| | Clean categories | Finds spelling variants of the same value (case, whitespace, accents) and merges them |\n\nActions that create a new dataset leave the original untouched and select the new dataset when done.\nThe calculations add a new column and leave the existing ones untouched; they build on SQL window functions and are\nexported to R as dplyr code with `group_by()` and `mutate()`.\nThe actions with regular expressions are easiest to try in the Regex tab in Analyse, where the matches show as the\npattern is typed; the pattern library and the pattern profile are there too (see [regex.md](regex.md)). The same\nJavaScript syntax applies in *Find and replace*, where `(?i)` at the start of the pattern or the choice *Ignore case*\nmakes the search case-insensitive.\n\n## Undo\n\nThe **Undo** button takes back the latest step, up to ten steps back within the same session. Cell edits are undone\nby writing back the old value; other actions by restoring a snapshot of the dataset. For very large datasets the\nsnapshot takes a while and at most 80 MB of snapshots are kept in memory. Undo after reloading the page is done with\nversions.\n\n## Data quality\n\nThe **Data quality** button profiles the dataset: number of rows and duplicates, and per column missing values,\ndistinct values, range and findings. The findings point at an action that fixes them in one click:\n\n- whitespace at the start or end, double spaces: Trim whitespace\n- empty texts that are not missing values: Replace value (empty to missing)\n- numbers or dates stored as text: Change type\n- probable encoding damage: Repair character encoding\n- values that differ only in case: Change case\n- values that look like personal identity numbers: Pseudonymise\n- column with the same value on every row (information)\n\n## Rules\n\nThe **Rules** button opens the dataset's quality rules: what must hold for the data to be correct. The rule types are:\n\n| Rule type | Broken by |\n|---|---|\n| Must not be missing | Missing values |\n| Unique | Values (or combinations of several columns) that occur more than once |\n| Within range | Numbers, dates or text outside the limits |\n| In list | Values that are not in the list |\n| No forbidden values | Values that are in the list, for example `unknown` or `-99` |\n| Matches pattern | Text that does not match the regular expression |\n| Has a format | Text that is not an e-mail address, a web address, an ISO date, a postcode (Swedish, Danish, Norwegian, Finnish or Icelandic) or a Swedish municipality or county code |\n| Text length | Text that is shorter or longer than the limits |\n| No surplus spaces | Text with spaces at the start or end, or double spaces |\n| Can be read as a type | Text that cannot be read as an integer, decimal number, date, date and time or yes/no |\n| Valid identity number | Numbers with the wrong form, an invalid date or a wrong check digit, see below |\n| Compare two columns | Rows where the condition between two columns does not hold, for example that the start date is less than or equal to the end date |\n| Found in another dataset | Values missing from a column in another dataset (referential integrity, like a foreign key) |\n| Share of filled-in values | The column as a whole, when the share of filled-in values is below the threshold (for example 95 %) |\n| No outliers | Numbers further from the quartiles than a chosen number of interquartile ranges (default 1.5), or further from the mean than a chosen number of standard deviations (default 3) |\n| SQL condition | Rows where the free SQL condition is not true |\n\nThe identity numbers are checked like this: Swedish personal identity number and coordination number (10 or 12\ndigits, valid date, day plus 60 for coordination numbers, check digit by Luhn), Swedish organisation number (third\ndigit at least 2, Luhn), Norwegian fødselsnummer and D-number (both check digits by modulus 11), Danish CPR number\n(valid date; modulus 11 is not checked because it has not applied to all numbers since 2007), Finnish personal\nidentity code (century sign and check character) and Icelandic kennitala (check digit by modulus 11, valid date,\ncentury digit). The check only says that the number can exist, not that it belongs to anyone.\n\n**Suggest rules** adds rules from the data quality profile: columns without missing values, unique columns and codes\nof fixed length. Columns whose names contain personnummer, organisationsnummer, CPR, fødselsnummer, henkilötunnus or\nkennitala get a rule for the kind of identity number that at least half of the values pass, columns with postnummer\nin the name a rule for the postcode format most values follow, and columns with e-mail (or e-post) in the name a rule\nfor e-mail addresses.\n\n**Run rules** counts the rows that break each rule, shows the share and some examples, and logs the run in the\ndataset's history. For a broken rule the failing rows can be saved as a dataset of their own, and the whole result\ncan be downloaded as an HTML report. The rules are stored in the dataset's metadata and therefore travel with\nbackups, bundles and the portable folder.\n\n## Pseudonymise\n\nThe action **Pseudonymise** (group Values, also in the column menu and the command palette) replaces identifiers\nsuch as personal identity numbers or customer numbers before sharing. Three methods:\n\n- **Hash**: SHA-256 of a salt and the value, shortened to any length (default 16 characters). The same value gives\n  the same hash, so joins between tables survive if the same salt is used. The salt is a secret text that you\n  choose; it is never stored in the log or the recipe (there it reads `***`), and a recipe with such a step cannot\n  be replayed without giving the salt again.\n- **Mask characters**: keeps any number of characters at the start and end plus separators, and replaces the rest\n  with `*` (`19800101-1234` becomes `198001**-****` with six characters at the start).\n- **Running number**: each distinct value gets a running number with a prefix in sorted order (`P0001`, `P0002`).\n\nHashing with a salt is pseudonymisation, not anonymisation: whoever knows the salt can test candidates, and\ncombinations of other columns may still single out persons. Remove or coarsen other sensitive columns, and never\nshare the salt together with the data. The recipe export to SQL uses Dataspatium's functions `dm_hash` and\n`dm_mask`; the export to R uses the package digest for hashes.\n\n## Geodata\n\nThe action group *Geodata* works with geometry columns (GeoJSON or WKT as text, in longitude/latitude or\nSWEREF 99 TM) and coordinate columns:\n\n- **Centroid from geometry** adds longitude and latitude (WGS 84) for the centre of each area or line, for example to\n  draw municipalities as bubbles in a point map.\n- **Area from geometry** adds the area in square kilometres (planar for SWEREF 99 TM, on the globe for\n  longitude/latitude; holes are subtracted).\n- **Reproject coordinates** converts two columns between SWEREF 99 TM (east, north) and WGS 84 (longitude,\n  latitude) into new columns, with Lantmäteriet's formulas.\n- **Municipality and county from coordinates** looks up which Swedish municipality and county each point lies in\n  with SCB's bundled boundaries and adds `kommunkod`, `kommun`, `lanskod` and `lan` (or only the county).\n  Coordinates in SWEREF 99 TM are recognised. The boundaries are SCB's simplified land boundaries for thematic maps:\n  points in lakes, the sea or outside Sweden become empty, and points very close to a boundary may end up in the\n  neighbouring municipality.\n\nAll four are recipe steps that can be undone and replayed. In the SQL export they appear as calls to Dataspatium's\nfunctions (`dm_geo_lon`, `dm_geo_area`, `dm_to_lon`, `dm_region` and others); the R export gives corresponding code\nwith the package sf.\n\n## Versions\n\nThe **Versions** button (also in Manage) saves a snapshot of the dataset with a description. A version can be\ncompared with the current state (number of rows, added, removed and retyped columns), restored or deleted. Versions\nare stored in the browser's database and come along in the workspace backup. Save a version before larger changes.\n\n## Recipe\n\nThe **Recipe** button shows all steps done with the dataset, in order. From here you can:\n\n- copy the recipe as R code (`dm_read()` with the short name, dplyr/tidyr/stringr calls, `dm_write()`) or as SQL\n  against the table `data`,\n- download the recipe as JSON to share with others,\n- run the same steps on another dataset in the workspace,\n- run a recipe from a JSON file on the selected dataset.\n\nReplay stops at the first step that fails (for example a column that does not exist) and tells how many steps were\nperformed. The recipe file has the form\n\n```json\n{ \"app\": \"Dataspatium\", \"kind\": \"recipe\", \"version\": \"0.3.0\", \"name\": \"Cleaning municipalities\",\n  \"steps\": [ { \"op\": \"trim\", \"params\": { \"columns\": [\"kommun\"], \"squish\": true }, \"description\": \"Trim kommun\" } ] }\n```\n\n## Code steps\n\nThe **Code** group in the palette has two actions that run a function on the whole table: **Python step**\n(`def step(df):` with pandas, see [python.md](python.md)) and **R step** (`function(df) { ... }`, see\n[r-guide.md](r-guide.md)). What the function returns becomes the new table, with types from the result; labels and\nunits follow columns that keep their name. *Preview* runs the function on the first 200 rows and shows the columns\nand a few rows. The step is logged in the recipe with its code, can be undone and replayed on other datasets; in the\nSQL export the code is a comment, in the R export the R step runs as code and the Python step is a comment. Python\nis fetched the first time.\n\n## The SQL console\n\nEvery dataset has a **short name** (shown below the name in the object panel and under Manage, where it can be changed\ntogether with the name): lower-case letters a–z, digits and underscores, at most 20 characters and preferably at most\n15, never a reserved word in SQL or R. The suggestion is formed from the name without filler words and with a year as\nsuffix: *Population per municipality 2024* becomes `population_2024`. The short names of the bundled example data are\nfixed in every language (`befolkning_2024`, `kommungranser`, `lansgranser`), so shared charts and recipes find them.\nIn the SQL console the selected dataset is called `data` but is also reached by its short name, and other datasets\nare reached by their short names, so that one query can join several datasets:\n\n```sql\nselect k.kommun, k.lan, b.folkmangd\nfrom befolkning_2024 b\njoin kommungranser k on k.kommunkod = b.kommunkod\norder by b.folkmangd desc\n```\n\nOther datasets are read-only in the query; UPDATE, DELETE and ALTER apply to the selected dataset but may read the\nothers (for example `update data set lan = (select lan from kommungranser k where k.kommunkod = data.kommunkod)`).\nAt most nine other datasets can take part in the same query. The result can be saved as a new dataset. The short\nnames also work in R (`dm_read(\"shortname\")`; the button *Insert dm_read()*, the R examples, new notebooks and the\nrecipes' R export use the short name) and are used when charts and notebooks are linked to datasets at import.\n\nThe SQL console is in Analyse mode under the SQL tab and works against the selected dataset, whose table is called\n`data`. Ctrl+Enter runs the whole script, selected text runs on its own.\n\n- `SELECT`, `WITH`, `VALUES`, `PRAGMA` and `EXPLAIN` show the result in a table (the first 5,000 rows).\n  **Save the result as a dataset** creates a new dataset from the whole result, without a row limit.\n- `UPDATE`, `DELETE`, `ALTER TABLE` and other statements change the dataset, are logged as the recipe step SQL\n  statement and can be undone in Process.\n- The properties panel lists the dataset's columns (a click inserts the name), the short names of all datasets and\n  the extra SQL functions Dataspatium adds: `regexp`, `regexp_replace`, `dm_parse_number`, `dm_parse_date`,\n  `dm_fix_encoding`, `dm_upper`, `dm_lower`, `dm_title`, `dm_squish`, `dm_round`, `dm_pad`, `dm_date_part`, `dm_date_diff` and\n  `dm_valid_id` (for example `select * from data where dm_valid_id(personnummer, 'se_pnr') = 0`), and the regex\n  functions `dm_regex_test`, `dm_regex_extract`, `dm_regex_all`, `dm_regex_count`, `dm_regex_split` and `dm_shape`\n  described in [regex.md](regex.md).\n- SQL scripts are saved in the object panel in the same way as R scripts.\n\nThe SQLite dialect applies: `||` for text concatenation, `cast(x as integer)`, `strftime()` for dates,\n`group_concat()`, window functions such as `row_number() over (partition by …)`.\n\n## Limitations\n\n- Undo applies within the session; use versions to go back after reloading.\n- Actions that rebuild a column (Change type, Split column, pivoting) export the dataset for undo first. On datasets\n  above a few hundred MB that takes noticeable time.\n- The recipes' R export covers the built-in actions; free SQL is exported as a comment since it cannot be translated\n  to R automatically.\n");
Dataspatium.assets.register("docs/en/visualisera.md","text","# Visualise\n\nVisualise mode turns datasets into charts with three engines that all use the app's palette, font and number\nformat: a chart builder (Vega-Lite), ready-made D3 templates, R scripts (ggplot2) and Python scripts (matplotlib, plotly). Charts are saved in the\nworkspace, can be exported as SVG, PNG and PDF, shared as files and created in batch per group.\n\n## Create a chart\n\n1. Choose a dataset in the object panel and press **New chart**.\n2. Choose an engine: **Chart builder**, **D3 template**, **R script** or **Python script**.\n3. Give the chart a name in the title field and press **Save**. Saved charts are listed under Charts in the object\n   panel and in the gallery.\n\nThe preview updates as you make changes. Below the chart it says how many rows were used; large datasets are\naggregated in SQL when the chart only needs sums, means or counts, otherwise the number of rows is limited (default\n50,000, changed under More options).\n\n## The chart builder (Vega-Lite)\n\n| Chart type | Used for |\n|---|---|\n| Bars, Horizontal bars | Comparing categories, with optional colour split and stacking |\n| Line, Area | Development over time or ordered categories |\n| Points | Relationship between two numbers, colour and size as extra dimensions |\n| Histogram | Distribution of a number in bins |\n| Heat map | Two categories with a value as colour |\n| Box plot | Spread per category |\n| Pie | Shares of a whole (few categories) |\n| Map | Areas coloured by a value (choropleth map) from a geometry column, see *Maps* below |\n| Point map | Points from longitude and latitude (or SWEREF 99 TM) with size and colour by values, over municipality or county boundaries |\n\nFor each channel (X, Y, Colour, Size, Small multiples) you choose a column, how it should be interpreted (number,\ncategory, ordered category, time), calculation (sum, mean, median, minimum, maximum, count, distinct count),\nsorting, time unit and axis title. Other settings: title and subtitle, number format, height, legend position,\nstacking (stacked, percent, overlapping), value labels on bars and points on lines. **Filters** limit the rows with\nconditions on columns. Under **More options** there are the row limit, colour scale (sequential or diverging) and\n**Custom Vega-Lite specification**: the JSON specification can be edited directly for anything the builder does not\ncover; data is inserted from the dataset when the chart is rendered.\n\nThe chart is saved as the builder's state (or the custom specification) together with the dataset's name and short\nname, so it can be recreated on new data with the same columns.\n\n### Axes and lines\n\nThe **Axes and lines** section (all chart types except pie and maps) controls the axes and adds lines. Empty fields\ngive automatic values.\n\n| Setting | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| Value axis minimum and maximum | Fixed limits for the value axis (Y, or X in horizontal bars), for example 0–100 for percentages or the same scale in several charts. Whatever falls outside the limits is clipped |\n| X axis minimum and maximum | Shown when the X axis is a number (points and lines), for example to zoom in on an interval |\n| Value axis scale | Linear, logarithmic (for values spanning several powers of ten; requires positive values) or square root |\n| Start the value axis at zero | On by default; turn it off to let the axis start near the smallest value, for example for temperatures |\n| Label angle | Automatic, 0°, 45° or 90° for categories with long names |\n| Gridlines | The grid behind the chart, on by default |\n| Reference line at value | A dashed line at a value on the value axis, for example a target or a national average, with optional text |\n| Trend line | Points and lines with a number on the X axis: straight line (linear regression) or smoothed curve (loess), one per colour group |\n| Opacity | How opaque points, areas and bars are (0.05–1), for example for many overlapping points |\n\nReference and trend lines are not drawn in charts with small multiples. The settings are saved with the chart and\ntravel with shared chart files and batch runs.\n\n**List** below the filters picks a saved search from Search that the chart's rows are taken from (the choice is\nshown only when the dataset has lists). The list runs again every time the chart is drawn, so the chart follows\ntoday's data, and the filters apply on top. The choice is saved with the chart and applies in dashboards and\nexports too.\n\n## D3 templates\n\n- **Ranking**: one row per category sorted by value, with a dot and a line. Choose category, value, calculation,\n  number and order.\n- **Slope chart**: two periods per category with a line between them; green lines increase, red decrease.\n- **Population pyramid**: groups (ages) as bars in two directions, for example women and men.\n\nThe templates are written in D3 with the app's style module and produce vector graphics that are exported unchanged.\n\n## R scripts\n\nThe script runs in the R session with the dataset loaded as `df` (read by its short name). Draw with ggplot2 or base\ngraphics; `theme_dataspatium()` and `dm_palette()` give the app's look. The latest chart is shown and saved with the\nscript. Export goes through R (svglite for SVG, `png()` and `pdf()`), and batch runs run the script once per group\nwith `df` filtered. Ctrl+Enter runs the script.\n\n## Python scripts\n\nThe script runs in the Python session with the dataset loaded as `df` (pandas). Draw with matplotlib (`fig, ax =\nplt.subplots()`) or plotly (`fig.show()`); `theme_dataspatium()` is applied automatically. The latest chart is shown\nand saved with the script; a plotly chart is also saved as a figure and shown interactively in dashboards and\npublished HTML. Python starts on *Start Python* or *Run* (fetched the first time, see [python.md](python.md)). Export\nand batch per group work as for R scripts.\n\n## Export\n\n**Export** opens a dialog with format (PNG, SVG, PDF, PowerPoint), width, height, scale for PNG and file name. PNG is\ndrawn in the browser with the app's font. SVG contains the font embedded so that the file looks the same on other\ncomputers. PDF is vector graphics (jsPDF and svg2pdf.js); text is set in the PDF standard font Helvetica. PowerPoint\n(pptx, through PptxGenJS) gives a presentation with a title slide and the chart as a high-resolution image; in Export\nmode there is also **All charts as PowerPoint**, one slide per saved chart. Charts can also be exported from Export\nmode, where all saved charts are listed.\n\n## Batch per group\n\n**Batch per group** creates the chart once per value in a grouping column (for example each municipality or year),\nfiltered on the value and with the value in the title, and packs all files into a ZIP together with `manifest.json`.\nChoose column, format (one or more), file name pattern (`{diagram}` and `{grupp}` are replaced by the chart name and\nthe group value), width and the maximum number of groups. The run can be cancelled. Groups that fail are listed in\nthe manifest. The PowerPoint format gives a single presentation with one slide per group instead of a ZIP.\n\n## Gallery and panel\n\n**Gallery** shows all saved charts as cards with thumbnail, dataset and time. Choose the number of columns, and press\n**Render all (panel)** to draw the charts live side by side. From here charts are opened, exported and deleted.\n\n## Maps\n\nTwo chart types draw maps, both without a map server: **Map** (choropleth: areas coloured by a value) and **Point\nmap** (points at coordinates, with size and colour by values). Municipality and region boundaries for Sweden,\nDenmark, Norway and Finland ship with the app, so a map of their municipalities or regions needs no download.\nBoundaries for other countries are imported from GeoJSON, shapefile or GeoPackage.\n\n![Choropleth map of population per municipality and point map of measuring stations in a dashboard](../images/kartor.png)\n\n### Choropleth map (Map)\n\n1. Get the boundaries: choose for example **Swedish municipality boundaries (SCB)** or **Norwegian municipality\n   boundaries (Kartverket)** in the list *Boundaries for maps* in Import and press **Import boundaries**. They become\n   datasets with code, name and a geometry column (`geometry`, GeoJSON in longitude and latitude). The codes are the\n   official ones: four-digit municipality codes in Sweden, Denmark (DAGI, such as `0101`; Statistics Denmark writes\n   `101`) and Norway, three digits in Finland (such as `091`; StatFin writes `KU091`). If the codes in the statistics\n   differ, they can be matched in Process with *Pad with leading zeros* or *Find and replace*. Your own boundaries are imported from GeoJSON, shapefile (for example SCB's *Digitala gränser* in\n   SWEREF 99 TM; simplify with a tolerance of 100 metres) or GeoPackage.\n2. Join the values to the boundaries in Process (*Join*) on the municipality or county code, so that the dataset has\n   both `geometry` and the value to colour. Alternatively, the values were already imported together with the\n   geometry.\n3. Choose *Map* in the chart builder. The geometry column is found automatically (the column `geometry`), the label\n   is a text column shown when you point at an area, and the value controls the colour with the app's sequential\n   scale (choose diverging under *More options* for deviations around a midpoint).\n\nThe geometry column may contain GeoJSON or WKT (`POLYGON((...))`, `POINT(...)` and others, also with a `SRID=...;`\nprefix), so that exports from GeoPackage, PostGIS and spreadsheets work directly.\n\n### Point map\n\nChoose *Point map* for a dataset with coordinates, for example weather stations or addresses with longitude and\nlatitude. The columns are found by name (`longitude`/`latitude`, `lon`/`lat`, `x`/`y`, `öst`/`norr`) and can be\nchanged. Coordinates in metres (SWEREF 99 TM) are recognised and converted to longitude and latitude. **Size** and\n**Colour** take value columns (or a category for the colour), **Label** is shown when you point at a point and\n**Background** chooses one of the bundled boundary sets or no background. If the dataset has areas instead\nof points (for example municipalities), *Process > Geodata > Centroid from geometry* gives longitude and latitude for\neach area, so that values can be drawn as bubbles per municipality.\n\n### Projection and boundaries\n\nThe projection is chosen from the coordinates: metres (SWEREF 99 TM) are drawn as they are, longitude and latitude\nwithin Europe through a transverse Mercator around the grid meridian of the country where the data lies (9° east for\nDenmark, 15° east for Sweden and Norway, 27° east for Finland) and others through Mercator. The bundled boundaries\nare simplified for thematic maps and not intended for exact boundary work: SCB's municipality and county boundaries\n(CC0, version February 2026), DAGI 1:500 000 for Denmark, Kartverket's N1000 for Norway (cut along the coastline,\nsince Norwegian municipalities otherwise reach out to sea) and Statistics Finland's 1:1 000 000 division for Finland\n(see `presets/geo/KALLA.md`). Maps are exported, shared, placed in dashboards and run in batch like every other chart\nin the builder; filters in dashboards work on maps too. A dataset with geometry can be exported as GeoJSON from Export.\n\n## Dashboards\n\nA dashboard is a named selection of saved charts in a grid with shared filters. Create one with **New dashboard** in\nthe object panel, give it a name and click **Add chart** to choose among the saved charts. Each card can be made\nwider (one, two or three columns), moved up or down, opened in the builder or removed from the dashboard; the number\nof grid columns and a description are set in the properties panel. All changes are saved automatically.\n\n**Filters** shows the columns that exist in every dataset the dashboard's charts use (text, integer, date and\nyes/no). Each chosen filter becomes a selector above the charts, and the choice applies to all Vega and D3 charts at\nonce (R and Python charts are shown as saved images, plotly charts interactively). The filters are added to the charts' own filters.\n\nThe dashboard is exported as standalone **HTML** (the charts as embedded SVG, with the active filters stated), as\n**PowerPoint** (title slide plus one slide per chart) or shared as `*.dash.json`, a file that contains the charts'\ndefinitions. At import the charts are linked to existing charts with the same name and dataset; those missing are\nrecreated from the file. The backup and the portable folder contain all dashboards. Dashboards can also be opened\nthrough the command palette (Ctrl+K) and exported from Export mode.\n\n**Present** shows the dashboard in the whole window, on a large screen or a projector: the button is in the\ndashboard's title row and the command *Present: name* in the command palette. The overview shows all charts; the\nright and left arrows (or space and Page Up/Page Down) go one chart at a time at full size, Home shows the overview\nagain, F toggles full screen where the browser allows it and Esc ends. The shared filters stay in the bar at the top\nand apply to every chart. Nothing in the dashboard changes during the presentation.\n\n## Sharing charts\n\n**Export configuration** downloads the chart as `*.viz.json` without data. Another user adds the file with **Import\nchart (file)**; the chart is linked to a dataset with the same short name or name in that workspace. The workspace\nbackup (Manage) contains all charts as such files.\n\n## Limitations\n\n- The charts live in the browser; very large datasets without aggregation are limited to the row limit.\n- PDF uses Helvetica, not Inter, for text.\n- R and Python charts run in their engine for export, batch, documents and PowerPoint; that takes a few seconds per chart, and Python is fetched the first time.\n- The bundled boundaries are simplified for thematic maps, not exact boundaries; fetch exact boundaries from\n  Lantmäteriet (or the corresponding agency) and import them as GeoPackage or shapefile.\n- PowerPoint export uses images (PNG) of the charts, not editable PowerPoint charts.\n");
Dataspatium.assets.register("docs/en/anteckningsbok.md","text","# Notebook\n\nA notebook mixes text, R code, Python code and SQL in cells that run in order, with the output saved under each cell. It is\nthe third tab in Analyse mode and is the way from analysis to finished report without leaving the app.\n\n## Create and work\n\nClick the plus sign next to *Notebooks* in the object panel (or choose *New notebook* in the command palette),\ngive the notebook a name and start in the first cell. A new notebook gets a heading cell, an R cell that reads the\nselected dataset by its short name and an SQL cell. The dataset the SQL cells run against is chosen in the\nproperties panel on the right.\n\nEach cell has a type (Text, R or SQL) that can be changed, buttons to run, move up or down and delete, and a result\narea. Ctrl+Enter runs the cell you are in; *Run all* runs the notebook from top to bottom. Cells are added at the\nbottom with the buttons *Text*, *R*, *Python* and *SQL*. Everything is saved automatically, output included.\n\n- **Text cells** are written in Markdown (headings with `#`, lists with `-`, `**bold**`, `*italic*`, links and\n  tables) and shown formatted below the editing field.\n- **R cells** run in the same R session as the R console, so variables live on between cells and runs until R is\n  restarted. Printed output, errors, charts and datasets created with `dm_write()` are gathered below the cell.\n  Charts are saved as PNG.\n- **Python cells** run in the same Python session as the Python console (Python is fetched the first time, see\n  [python.md](python.md)). Output, errors, matplotlib and plotly charts and datasets from `dm_write()` are collected under the cell.\n- **SQL cells** run against the table `data` in the notebook's dataset. SELECT queries show up to 1,000 rows (the\n  first 50 in the view); UPDATE, DELETE and other modifying statements are performed as a recipe step in the\n  dataset and logged there.\n\n## Export and share\n\n*Export* creates a document from the whole notebook: HTML (standalone page), Word, PDF, Markdown (with figures in a\nZIP when charts are included) or LaTeX (ZIP with `main.tex` and figures). Text cells become body text, code becomes\ncode blocks (can be left out), tables become tables and charts become images. The same builders are used as for\nthe document templates (see [dokumentmallar.md](dokumentmallar.md)).\n\n*Share (file)* downloads the notebook as `*.nb.json` with cells and saved output; another user loads the file with\n*Import notebook (file)*, and the dataset is linked by its short name or name. Notebooks also travel in the backup,\nin bundles and in the portable folder.\n\n## Limitations\n\n- R code cannot be interrupted under file://; use *Restart R* in the R tab if a cell hangs. When the app runs from a\n  web server, the button *Stop* in the R tab also stops a running cell.\n- Output is stored in the browser together with the notebook. Many large charts make the notebook larger.\n- The text editing field has no syntax highlighting for Markdown.\n");
Dataspatium.assets.register("docs/en/regex.md","text","# Regular expressions (Regex)\n\nA regular expression is a pattern that describes text: `^\\d{3} ?\\d{2}$` describes a Swedish postcode,\n`\\(([^)]*)\\)` a text in parentheses. In Dataspatium the patterns are used to analyse, correct and split values in\ndatasets. The **Regex** tab in the Analyse mode is the place to try patterns on a column and see the result at once.\nThe same patterns also work in Process (the action group *Regular expressions*, *Find and replace*), in the quality\nrule *Matches pattern* and in the SQL console.\n\n## Which standard?\n\nDataspatium uses JavaScript's regular expressions as defined by the ECMAScript standard (ECMA-262), with the\nUnicode flag on. The reasons:\n\n- They are built into the browser, so no extra library is needed and everything works from `file://`.\n- The same engine is used everywhere in the app: in the Regex tab, in the SQL functions of the SQLite engine, in\n  the rules and in Find and replace. A pattern that works in one place works in all of them.\n- They have what data work needs: named groups `(?<name>…)`, look-ahead and look-behind (`(?=…)`, `(?<=…)`),\n  Unicode classes such as `\\p{L}` (every letter, including å, ä, ö, æ, ø, þ and ð) and case flags.\n- The syntax is well documented (for example on MDN) and very close to that of the R package stringr (ICU) and\n  PCRE, so patterns move easily between Dataspatium, R and most other tools.\n\nOther standards that were compared: POSIX ERE (R's default engine TRE) lacks look-around and Unicode classes. PCRE\n(R with `perl = TRUE`) and ICU (stringr) are close relatives of JavaScript. RE2 guarantees that a pattern can never\nhang, but is not available in the browser and lacks back-references and look-around. The risk with JavaScript, that\nsome patterns can take a very long time, is handled with a time limit instead (see *Safety and performance*).\n\nDataspatium's rules on top of the standard:\n\n- The Unicode flag is added when the pattern allows it. An older pattern that is only valid without it, for\n  example `a\\-b`, still works, but then without `\\p{…}`.\n- `(?i)`, `(?s)` and `(?m)` at the start of the pattern become flags, as in R, Python and PCRE. `(?i)malmö`\n  matches MALMÖ.\n- `\\w`, `\\d` and `\\b` only cover a–z, A–Z, 0–9 and underscore. Use `\\p{L}` for letters, and `(?<!\\p{L})` and\n  `(?!\\p{L})` as a word boundary in text with å, ä and ö.\n\n## The Regex tab\n\nThe tab sits in Analyse next to R, SQL and Notebook and has the same layout as the rest of the app:\n\n- **Left**: the datasets, your saved patterns and the pattern library.\n- **Centre**: column, pattern, flags and what to do, with a preview.\n- **Right**: the syntax in brief and the same thing as SQL and R code, which can be opened in the SQL or R tab.\n\nHow to use it:\n\n1. Choose a dataset in the list on the left and a column in the **Column** field.\n2. Type a pattern in the **Pattern** field, or click a pattern in the library. The preview shows the column's most\n   common values (up to 500 distinct ones) with the matches marked, and the line below the field tells how many\n   rows match.\n3. Choose **Ignore case**, **The whole value must match** (the pattern is wrapped in `^(?:` and `)$`) and **Dot\n   matches line breaks** as needed.\n4. Choose what to do under **What to do** and press **Apply**.\n\n| What to do | What happens |\n|---|---|\n| Test and show matches | Shows the matches, the number per value and, if you like, only values with or without a match. Nothing changes |\n| Pattern profile | Shows the shapes of the values: `A` is an upper-case letter, `a` another letter and `9` a digit, so `114 55` has the shape `999 99`. *Simplify repetitions* writes a run as `9+`. **Use** makes a pattern from the shape. Nothing changes |\n| Filter rows | Keeps the rows that match, or removes them |\n| Extract into new columns | A new column per group in the pattern, or one column with the whole match if the pattern has no groups. Named groups give the column names; other names can be given. *All matches* collects every match with a separator |\n| Replace | Replaces the matches in text columns. `$1`, `$2` and `$<name>` insert a group, `$&` the whole match |\n| Split into columns | Splits the value where the pattern matches, into a chosen number of columns; the last one gets the rest of the text |\n| Flag matches in a new column | A yes/no column that shows whether the value matches |\n| Count matches in a new column | A column with the number of matches in each value |\n| Create a quality rule | Adds the rule *Matches pattern* for the column; it runs under Rules in Process |\n\nWhatever changes the dataset becomes a recipe step, just like the actions in Process: it can be undone there,\nreplayed on another dataset and exported as SQL (with Dataspatium's `dm_regex_*` functions) and as R code (with the\npackage stringr). The same steps are in Process under **Actions** in the group *Regular expressions*.\n\nValues that are not text, such as numbers and dates, are tried in their text form (`2.5`, `2024-01-31`).\n\n## The pattern library\n\nThe library has ready-made patterns in three groups. *Sweden*: personal identity number, organisation number,\npostcode, municipality code, phone number and car registration number. *Nordic countries*: Danish CPR number,\nNorwegian fødselsnummer, Finnish personal identity code, Icelandic kennitala and Nordic postcodes. *General*:\ne-mail address, web address in text, dates as YYYY-MM-DD and D.M.YYYY, time of day, year in text, number with\ndecimal comma, amount in kronor, text in parentheses, words, surplus spaces and hashtags.\n\nThe library patterns check the form, not whether a number is valid. Check digits and dates in identity numbers are\nchecked by the rule *Valid identity number* in Process.\n\n## Saved patterns\n\n**Save pattern** saves the pattern and the flags under a name. Saved patterns are listed on the left and, like R\nand SQL scripts, travel in the backup, in bundles (when *Saved scripts and patterns* is chosen) and in the portable\nfolder, as files `scripts/<id>.script.json`.\n\n## Syntax in brief\n\n| Pattern | Means | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| `.` | any character | `a.c` matches abc and a-c |\n| `\\d` | digit 0–9 | `\\d{4}` matches 2024 |\n| `\\p{L}`, `\\p{Lu}`, `\\p{Ll}` | letter, upper case, lower case | `\\p{Lu}\\p{Ll}+` matches Malmö |\n| `\\s` | space, tab or line break | `\\s{2,}` matches double spaces |\n| `[abc]`, `[^abc]`, `[a-z]` | one of the characters, none of them, a range | `[.,]` matches a full stop or a comma |\n| `a\\|b` | a or b | `kr\\|SEK` |\n| `*`, `+`, `?` | zero or more, one or more, zero or one | `\\d+` |\n| `{n}`, `{n,m}`, `{n,}` | exactly n, n to m, at least n times | `\\d{3,4}` |\n| `^`, `$` | start and end of the value | `^\\d+$` is digits only |\n| `(…)` | group, used as `$1` | `(\\d{3}) ?(\\d{2})` |\n| `(?<name>…)` | named group, used as `$<name>` | `(?<year>\\d{4})` |\n| `(?:…)` | group that is not captured | `(?:kr\\|SEK)` |\n| `(?=…)`, `(?!…)` | followed by, not followed by | `\\d+(?= kr)` |\n| `(?<=…)`, `(?<!…)` | preceded by, not preceded by | `(?<=no )\\d+` |\n| `\\` | makes a special character ordinary | `\\.` is a full stop, `\\(` a parenthesis |\n\n## Examples\n\n| Task | Pattern | What to do |\n|---|---|---|\n| Year from a text such as *Folkmängd 2024* | `(?<year>(?:19\\|20)\\d{2})` | Extract; new column `year` |\n| County code in parentheses, *Stockholms län (01)* | `\\((\\d{2})\\)` | Extract; give the name `county_code` |\n| Postcode with a space | `^(\\d{3}) ?(\\d{2})$` | Replace with `$1 $2` |\n| Surname and first name from *Berg, Anna* | `, ?` | Split into 2 columns |\n| Rows with an e-mail address in free text | `[^\\s@]+@[^\\s@]+\\.[^\\s@]+` | Flag matches or Filter rows |\n| Mixed date formats | (none) | Pattern profile shows for example `9999-99-99` and `99/99/9999`; then fix with Change type in Process |\n| Phone numbers with digits only | `[^\\d+]` | Replace with nothing (or the action Remove characters) |\n\n## In SQL and R\n\nThe SQL console has the same patterns through Dataspatium's functions:\n\n| Function | Gives |\n|---|---|\n| `x regexp 'pattern'`, `regexp('pattern', x)` | 1 if x matches, otherwise 0 |\n| `regexp_replace(x, 'pattern', 'replacement', 'gi')` | x with the matches replaced (`g` all, `i` ignoring case) |\n| `dm_regex_test(x, 'pattern', 'i')` | 1, 0 or empty for an empty value |\n| `dm_regex_extract(x, 'pattern', group, '')` | the group (number or name, 0 for the whole match) of the first match |\n| `dm_regex_all(x, 'pattern', group, ', ', '')` | the group of every match with a separator |\n| `dm_regex_count(x, 'pattern', '')` | the number of matches |\n| `dm_regex_split(x, 'pattern', part, parts, '')` | part number `part` of `parts` when x is split at the pattern |\n| `dm_shape(x, 0)` | the shape in the pattern profile (1 simplifies repetitions) |\n\n```sql\nselect kommun, dm_regex_extract(namn, '\\((\\d{2})\\)', 1, '') as county_code\nfrom data\nwhere dm_regex_test(postnr, '^\\d{3} ?\\d{2}$', '') = 0\n```\n\nIn R the package stringr is used, whose syntax (ICU) is almost the same: `stringr::str_detect(df$postnr,\nstringr::regex(\"^\\\\d{3} ?\\\\d{2}$\"))`. The differences to know: in R every `\\` is written twice inside a string, a\ngroup in a replacement is written `\\\\1` instead of `$1` (the recipe export converts it for you), and look-behind must\nhave a bounded length. `\\d` matches every Unicode digit in ICU but only 0–9 in JavaScript.\n\n## Safety and performance\n\nSome patterns can take an extremely long time on some values, for example `(a+)+$` on a long run of a followed by\nanother character (*catastrophic backtracking*). Patterns are therefore tried in a background thread of their own\nwith a time limit of two seconds. When the time runs out the thread is stopped, a message is shown and nothing is\nchanged. Before a step changes a dataset, the pattern is tried on every value of the column in the same way. In\nthat case make the pattern simpler: avoid nested repetitions such as `(a+)+` and `(\\w*)*`, and be as specific as\npossible, for example `[^,]*` instead of `.*`.\n\nThe preview shows the 500 most common distinct values. The number of matches is counted exactly when the column\nhas at most 20,000 distinct values, otherwise over the 20,000 most common ones, which is then stated below the\npattern field.\n\n## Limitations\n\n- Replace works on text columns; change the type to text first for other columns.\n- A recipe replayed on another dataset tries the pattern on that dataset's values before it runs.\n- The R export uses stringr. Unusual syntax, such as look-behind of unbounded length, can give a different result\n  in R.\n");
Dataspatium.assets.register("docs/en/format.md","text","# Formats and import\n\n## Supported file formats\n\n| Format | Import | Export | Comment |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| CSV, TSV, TXT with delimiters | Yes | Yes | Delimiter, character encoding and header row are detected automatically and can be changed |\n| Fixed-width text | Yes | No | Choose the format in the import step (files named .fwf or .prn are recognised). Column starts are found from spaces present on every line, or given as positions |\n| Excel xlsx, xlsm, xls, xlsb | Yes | xlsx | Choose sheet; dates and yes/no become real types |\n| ODS and FODS (LibreOffice) | Yes | ods | Same type handling as Excel |\n| Older spreadsheet formats: Excel 2003 XML, SYLK (slk), DIF, dBase (dbf), Lotus 1-2-3 (wk1, wk3, wk4, wks), Quattro Pro (wq1, wq2, wb1, wb2, wb3, qpw) | Yes | No | Read by the same spreadsheet reader as Excel; choose sheet. Excel 2003 XML is recognised by its content even when the file is called .xml |\n| Tables in documents: HTML (html, htm, xhtml), Markdown (md), Word (docx), LaTeX (tex) | Yes | No | All tables in the file are listed with their heading or caption and their number of rows and columns. Merged cells (colspan and rowspan, Word's merged cells, `\\multicolumn`) are filled in on every row and column they cover. The header row comes from `th`, `thead`, Markdown's delimiter row, Word's header row or `booktabs` (`\\midrule`) |\n| KML and KMZ (Google Earth) | Yes | No | One row per place with name, description, folder path, time and extended data as columns; points, lines, polygons and multi-geometries become GeoJSON |\n| GPX (GPS tracks) | Yes | No | Choose a layer: waypoints, routes, tracks (with number of points and length in km) or track points (one row per point with time and elevation) |\n| TopoJSON | Yes | No | Each object in the topology is a layer; the arcs are decoded into ordinary GeoJSON geometries. Recognised by its content even when the file is called .json |\n| JSON | Yes | Yes | List of objects, an object containing a list (a path can be given), list of lists |\n| NDJSON, JSON Lines | Yes | No | One object per line |\n| JSON-stat | Yes | No | Recognised automatically (`class: dataset`, versions 1.x and 2.0); one row per cell with codes, texts and value |\n| SDMX-JSON | Yes | No | Data messages in SDMX-JSON 1.0 and 2.0 (for example from the ECB and the OECD) are recognised automatically; one row per observation with a code and text column per dimension, the time period, the value and the attributes |\n| GeoJSON | Yes | Yes | One row per feature; the properties become columns, the geometry a text column with GeoJSON that the map chart draws (the map also reads WKT text in a column, for example from CSV). At export the geometry column (GeoJSON or WKT) becomes each feature's geometry and the other columns its properties |\n| Shapefile (ZIP with shp, dbf, prj, cpg, or shp alone) | Yes | No | The attributes become typed columns according to the dbf schema, the geometry GeoJSON in the column `geometry`. Choose the layer if the ZIP file has several, and simplify the geometry with a tolerance in map units. The coordinates are kept (for example SWEREF 99 TM or longitude/latitude) |\n| XML | Yes | Yes | One row per repeated element (chosen in the import step); attributes and child elements become columns, nested elements get dotted names such as `lage.lan` |\n| SQLite | Yes | Yes | Dataspatium's own file is restored with types, labels and history; in other databases you choose a table and the types are taken from the schema |\n| SQL script (sql) | Yes | No | A script with `CREATE TABLE` and `INSERT`, for example a database dump, is run in an empty SQLite database and the tables are offered for selection. MySQL-specific lines (`SET`, `LOCK TABLES`, `USE`, `/*!…*/` and table options such as `ENGINE=`) are removed first; other dialects work as far as they follow SQLite's syntax |\n| GeoPackage (gpkg) | Yes | No | The layers (feature and attribute tables) are chosen as tables; the geometry column becomes GeoJSON that the map chart draws, the reference system is shown |\n| Access (mdb, accdb) | Yes | No | Read directly in the browser with mdb-reader; the table is chosen in the Settings step and the types come from Access. Queries, linked tables and passwords need the [database bridge](kallor.md). |\n| SPSS sav, zsav, por | Yes | sav | Through the R package haven. Variable labels become column labels and value labels column metadata (see below) |\n| Stata dta | Yes | dta | Through haven. Value labels as for SPSS. At export the column names are shortened to Stata's rules (32 characters, ASCII) |\n| SAS sas7bdat, xpt | Yes | No | Through haven |\n| PX (PC-Axis, used by the Nordic statistics offices) | Yes | No | Through the R package pxR; the character encoding is read from the file's CODEPAGE or CHARSET. The result is a long table with one value column |\n| Parquet | Yes | Yes | Through the R package nanoparquet; keeps types exactly |\n| RDS, RData | Yes | rds | R objects; an RData file must contain a data.frame |\n| Pasted text | Yes | | Tab-, semicolon- or comma-separated |\n\nDelimited text files of 64 MB or more are read in chunks: the preview shows the first rows as usual, and at import\nthe rows are written to the dataset while the file is read, so that the whole text never needs to be in memory. The\ncolumn types are taken from the column step or guessed from the first chunk. Progress is shown as the rows read.\n\nAll file formats can also be fetched from a web address under *From web address* in Import, if the server allows\nrequests from the browser (CORS). Dataspatium's own configuration files (API source, chart, dashboard, document\ntemplate, notebook) are recognised by the field `format` and added directly.\n\nThe formats that go through R start the R session when needed (a few seconds the first time) and have no preview:\nthe file is read by R and handed over with `dm_write()`, so the column types come from R. Yes/no becomes 0/1 in SPSS\nand Stata, which lack a logical type.\n\nValue labels (codes with names, such as `1 = Man` and `2 = Woman`) travel with the column as metadata in the SQLite\nfile (`_columns.value_labels`). When importing SPSS, Stata and SAS you choose in the settings step whether the column\nshows the labels as text (the default) or keeps the codes; the labels are kept either way. They can be set, changed\nand swapped for the codes in Process, are shown in the codebook, reach R as `haven::labelled` and are written to .sav\nand .dta, where a column with the labels as text gets its codes back.\n\nData that is added in Import also ships with the app: the example data *Population per municipality 2024 (Sweden)*\nand boundaries for maps as GeoJSON in longitude and latitude: municipalities and counties in Sweden (SCB, CC0),\nmunicipalities and regions in Denmark (DAGI), municipalities and counties in Norway (Kartverket, CC BY 4.0) and\nmunicipalities and regions in Finland (Statistics Finland, CC BY 4.0). Sources and licences are in\n`presets/geo/KALLA.md`. Nothing is fetched from the internet.\n\n## Character encoding\n\nText files are checked in this order: byte order mark (UTF-8, UTF-16), UTF-16 without BOM (many zero bytes), plain\nASCII, strict UTF-8 validation of the first megabyte, otherwise Windows-1252. Windows-1252 is common for files saved\nfrom older Nordic Excel versions. You can always change the encoding in the import step.\n\n## Delimiters and decimal separators\n\nThe delimiter is guessed among semicolon, comma, tab and pipe. Nordic CSV files usually use semicolons, since the\ncomma is the decimal separator. The decimal separator is decided per column: if comma decimals are at least as\ncommon as point decimals, comma is chosen. Thousands separators (space, non-breaking space, point with comma\ndecimals) are removed.\n\n## Column types\n\n| Type | Recognised as | Stored as |\n|---|---|---|\n| Text | Everything that fits nothing else, and all numbers with a leading zero (municipality codes, postcodes, personal identity numbers) | TEXT |\n| Integer | `12`, `-3`, `1 234` | INTEGER |\n| Decimal number | `1 234,5`, `1.5`, `2,0`, `1.5e3` | REAL |\n| Date | `2024-01-31`, `31/12/2024`, `31.12.2024`, `31-12-2024` | TEXT, ISO 8601 `YYYY-MM-DD` |\n| Date and time | `2024-01-31 10:05`, `2024-01-31T10:05:00Z`, Excel dates with time | TEXT, `YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS` |\n| Yes/no | `ja`, `nej`, `true`, `false`, `sant`, `falskt`, `j`, `n`, `yes`, `no` | INTEGER 0/1 |\n\nA column gets a type only if all filled-in values in the sample fit. Empty values always become \"empty\" (NULL). Values\nthat cannot be interpreted with a type you choose manually also become empty.\n\n## The dataset's file\n\nEach dataset is an SQLite file with the tables `data` (the rows), `_columns` (name, type, label, original name,\ndescription, unit and value labels; the last three are added when an older file is opened), `_meta` (name, short name,\ndescription, timestamps, source, import settings), `_log` (everything done with the dataset) and, once someone has\ncommented, `_comments` (time, name, text, column and whether the comment is resolved). The file can be opened in R\nwith RSQLite, in DB Browser for SQLite and in most other tools.\n\n## The workspace's files\n\nThe backup (ZIP), the portable folder (Edge and Chrome) and the folder in a GitHub repository have the same\nstructure: `manifest.json` with a list of the contents, `datasets/<id>.sqlite` per dataset, `charts/<id>.viz.json` per\nsaved chart, `documents/<id>.doc.json` per document template of your own, `dashboards/<id>.dash.json` per dashboard,\n`notebooks/<id>.nb.json` per notebook and `scripts/<id>.script.json` per saved R script, SQL script or regex pattern. A\nbundle (*Bundle* in the Share mode) has the same structure but contains only what you chose. Its manifest has\n`kind: bundle`, the sender's name (`author`), the message (`message`) and the changes in each dataset since it was last\nshared or received (`changes`), and the bundle also has `CHANGELOG.md` with the same and the unresolved comments in\nreadable form. When a bundle is loaded, the message and the changes are shown, and everything that already exists is\nskipped (datasets with the same id, charts, dashboards, templates and notebooks with the same name), except the\ndatasets you choose to replace with the bundle's version; your version is then kept under *Versions*. With automatic\nsaving on, changed datasets are written to the portable folder shortly after each save and files for deleted datasets\nare removed. The folder can be loaded on another computer in the same way as a bundle.\n\nThe manifest has `format: 2` since version 2.0: every dataset in the list has, besides `id`, `name` and `shortName`,\nalso `folder` (the folder, such as `Economy/2026`, or empty) and `tags` (a list). Older bundles and backups (without\n`format`) are read as before. Folder and tags are also in the dataset's own file (`_meta`), so they come along\nwherever the file goes, as does `kind` (`table`, `graph`, `document`, `images`, `model`); in 2.0 all datasets are\ntables.\n\nThe rows in `_log` have a timestamp, action type and details as JSON. The action types are among others `import`, `op`\n(a recipe step with `op`, `params`, `result` and `description`; these rows make up the recipe), `undo`, `snapshot`,\n`restore`, `comment` (a new comment), `shared` and `received` (when the dataset was shared or received in a bundle or\nfrom GitHub; the change log of the next bundle starts after the latest of them). Manage shows the rows as readable\ntext. The recipe export in Process builds on the `op` rows, see [bearbeta.md](bearbeta.md).\n");
Dataspatium.assets.register("docs/en/api-konfiguration.md","text","# Open APIs and API configurations\n\nDataspatium fetches data straight from open APIs into your browser. Nothing goes through any server: the requests go\nfrom your computer to the agency's service, and the response becomes a dataset in the workspace. That requires the\nservice to allow requests from browsers (CORS); all presets below have been checked for it.\n\n## Preset sources\n\nThe list in Import is grouped by country. The country buttons above it (Sweden, Denmark, Norway, Finland, Iceland,\nFaroe Islands, Greenland, Åland, Nordic region, EU, International) choose which countries' sources are shown; the choice is stored\nin the browser. The sources' names and descriptions follow the app's language, and PxWeb services available in\nseveral languages (SCB, Statistics Norway, Statistics Finland, Statistics Iceland, Statistics Greenland, ÅSUB) are\nbrowsed in the app's language when the service has it.\n\n### Sweden and the EU\n\n| Source | What | Key | How to fetch |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| SCB Statistical Database | Official statistics, the whole subject tree | No | Browse to a table, choose values per variable, Fetch |\n| SCB Statistical Database (API v2, beta) | The same statistics through SCB's new API | No | Search for a table, choose values, Fetch |\n| Public Health Agency of Sweden (Folkhälsodata) | Public health surveys, communicable diseases, vaccinations and more | No | Browse, choose values, Fetch |\n| Kolada | Key figures for municipalities and regions | No | Search for key figures (id), fetch values per year or per municipality |\n| Eurostat | EU statistics | No | Give the dataset code and filters |\n| European Central Bank (ECB) | Exchange rates and the ECB's statistics (SDMX) | No | Choose currencies and frequency for the exchange rates, or give the dataflow and series key |\n| OECD | Unemployment, GDP growth and the OECD's other statistics (SDMX) | No | Choose countries, or give the dataflow and series key |\n| National Board of Health and Welfare | Causes of death, diagnoses, medicines, operations and more | No | Choose subject, choose values per variable, Fetch |\n| SMHI meteorological observations | The weather stations' measurements | No | Choose parameter, station and period |\n| Air quality (Swedish EPA data host) | Air pollutants per measuring station | No | List time series, fetch values for a time series |\n| Nature Conservation Register (Swedish EPA) | Protected areas (WFS) | No | Choose layer, Fetch |\n| Lantmäteriet open geodata (catalogue) | Products and download files | No | List products and files, download, import as a file |\n| Sweden's data portal | Search the datasets of all agencies | No | Search, read the table, download and import as a file |\n| Lantmäteriet Direkt (place names) | Place name search | Yes, account in Geotorget (OAuth2) | Give client id and client secret, search |\n| Boverket energy performance certificates | Energy performance certificates for buildings | Yes, key from Boverket's API portal | Give the key, call according to the portal's documentation |\n\n### The other Nordic countries\n\nAll have been checked for CORS (requests with an `Origin` header) in November 2026 and need no key.\n\n| Country | Source | What | How to fetch |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Denmark | Statistics Denmark StatBank | Official statistics (api.statbank.dk) | Browse the subject tree, choose a table and values per variable; variables left out are summed away |\n| Denmark | Open Data DK | The data portal of Danish municipalities and regions | Search datasets, one row per file, import the file through From web address |\n| Norway | Statistics Norway StatBank (API v1 and v2) | Official statistics, Norwegian and English | Browse the subject tree or search for a table, choose values; 800,000 cells per request |\n| Norway | FHI Statistics | Public health statistics from the Norwegian Institute of Public Health's registers and statistics banks | Choose source, table and values per dimension (the measures in MEASURE_TYPE) |\n| Norway | Central Coordinating Register for Legal Entities (Brønnøysund) | Companies and organisations | Search by name, municipality, organisation form or industry code |\n| Norway | Norwegian Mapping Authority municipality info | Municipalities and counties | Fetch the lists, also municipalities per county |\n| Norway | MET Norway Locationforecast | Weather forecast per coordinate | Give latitude and longitude |\n| Finland | Statistics Finland StatFin | Official statistics, Finnish, Swedish and English | Browse the subject tree, choose values; 100,000 cells per request |\n| Finland | Municipal key figures (Statistics Finland) | Key figures per municipality and wellbeing services county | Browse, choose values |\n| Finland | Sotkanet (THL) | Indicators for health and welfare | Fetch indicators and regions, then values for an indicator and years |\n| Finland | YTJ company register (PRH) | Companies | Search by name, location, business ID or line of business |\n| Finland | Digitraffic road weather (Fintraffic) | Road weather stations and measurements | Fetch stations (point map) and the latest measurements |\n| Finland | Finnish Meteorological Institute | Weather observations per place or station | Give place or station id, parameters and period |\n| Finland | Avoindata.fi | Finland's data portal | Search datasets, one row per file |\n| Iceland | Statistics Iceland | Official statistics, Icelandic and English | Browse the databases, choose values; 10,000 cells per request |\n| Iceland | Icelandic Met Office | Weather observations per station | Give station numbers |\n| Greenland | Statistics Greenland | Official statistics, Danish and English | Browse, choose values |\n| Faroe Islands | Statistics Faroe Islands | Official statistics, English | Browse, choose values |\n| Åland | ÅSUB | Official statistics, Swedish and English | Browse, choose values |\n| Nordic region | Nordic Statistics database | Comparable Nordic statistics, English | Browse, choose values |\n\nNot included: Denmark's DMI and Norway's Frost (MET) require a key; Energi Data Service and data.norge.no lack CORS;\nopingogn.is no longer has a search API. Sources without CORS, for example the Riksbank and the World Bank, cannot be\nreached directly from the browser; they need your own proxy, see below. The Swedish Civil Defence and Resilience\nAgency (MCF, formerly MSB) has no documented open API; its statistics are imported as files.\n\n## How to do it\n\n1. Open **Import** mode and choose a source under **Open APIs** in the object panel. The country buttons above the\n   list limit it to chosen countries.\n2. If the source requires a key: give it under **Authentication**. Choose **Use in this session** (forgotten when the\n   page closes) or **Save encrypted** with a passphrase.\n3. Browse or search for what you want. For the PxWeb services (SCB, the Nordic statistics offices, the Public Health\n   Agency), Statistics Denmark, FHI and the National Board of Health and Welfare you choose values per variable; the\n   number of cells is shown, and selections above the service's limit are split into several requests\n   automatically. For the other sources you fill in the request's parameters. Lists (stations, municipalities,\n   products) are fetched from the service when you open the field.\n4. Press **Fetch**. Progress is shown and can be cancelled. **Preview the first page** fetches only one request.\n5. Review the table and press **Save as dataset**, or add the rows to an existing dataset (append or replace). The\n   dataset gets the source in its description: publisher, table, address and licence.\n\nThe properties panel shows publisher, licence, rate limit and the latest requests with status and response time.\n\n## Updating a fetched dataset\n\nDatasets that come from an API remember their source and selection. Under **Saved fetches** in Import they are\nlisted with source, last fetch and number of rows. **Update** fetches the same data again, replaces the rows and\nreplays the dataset's recipe, so that cleaning and computed columns come back; **Update all** does it for all of them\nin turn, and the command palette and Manage offer the same as **Re-fetch API datasets**. If the fetch or a recipe\nstep fails, the dataset is left as it was and the error is shown. A recipe step that needs a secret (for example a\nkey for pseudonymisation) cannot be replayed automatically. **Manage** also has **Fetch again**, where you choose\nbetween updating and putting the new rows last without running the recipe.\n\n## Keys and security\n\n- Keys are never stored in clear text. By default they exist only in memory during the session.\n- **Save encrypted** encrypts the key with a passphrase (PBKDF2 and AES-GCM) in the browser's database. The same\n  passphrase applies to all saved keys. The passphrase is not stored; if you forget it you must enter the keys again.\n- When the app is opened again, saved keys are locked until you give the passphrase (**Unlock saved keys**).\n- Keys are sent only to the address the configuration states and never come along when a configuration is exported or\n  the workspace is backed up.\n- Under file:// all locally opened HTML pages share storage in Chromium. That is why encryption is the default for\n  saved keys.\n\n### Getting keys\n\n- **Boverket**: register in [Boverket's API portal](https://api-portal.boverket.se/), subscribe to the API for energy\n  performance certificates and copy the subscription key. Dataspatium sends it in the header\n  `Ocp-Apim-Subscription-Key`. Boverket allows 10 requests per 2 seconds and 1,500 per day.\n- **Lantmäteriet Direkt**: create an account in [Geotorget](https://geotorget.lantmateriet.se/), add the product\n  Ortnamn Direkt (open data, free of charge) and create API keys. Give the client id (consumer key) and client secret\n  (consumer secret); Dataspatium fetches an access token from `https://api.lantmateriet.se/token` and renews it when\n  it expires.\n\n## Proxy for services without CORS\n\nUnder **More options** in the properties panel you can give the address of a proxy that you run yourself, for example\n`https://my-proxy.example/?url={url}`. `{url}` is replaced by the encoded target address; if `{url}` is missing the\ntarget address is appended at the end. Dataspatium ships no proxy and never sends anything to any server other than\nthe one you give.\n\n## Configuration format\n\nA source is described by a JSON file with the extension `.api.json`. Your own configurations are added with **Add API\nfrom file** and can be exported with **Export configuration** to be shared. The presets are in `presets/api/` in the\napp folder and are good starting points.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"format\": \"dataspatium-api/1\",\n  \"id\": \"my-service\",\n  \"name\": \"Min tjänst\",\n  \"publisher\": \"The agency\",\n  \"description\": \"What the service contains (Swedish is the base language of the file).\",\n  \"docs\": \"https://example.org/api-documentation\",\n  \"license\": { \"name\": \"CC0 1.0\", \"url\": \"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/\" },\n  \"level\": 1,\n  \"country\": \"SE\",\n  \"baseUrl\": \"https://api.example.org/v1\",\n  \"auth\": { \"type\": \"none\" },\n  \"rateLimit\": { \"requests\": 10, \"perSeconds\": 1 },\n  \"endpoints\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"list\",\n      \"name\": \"Lista poster\",\n      \"method\": \"GET\",\n      \"path\": \"/records/{kind}\",\n      \"params\": [\n        { \"name\": \"kind\", \"in\": \"path\", \"label\": \"Typ\", \"type\": \"enum\", \"required\": true,\n          \"options\": [{ \"value\": \"a\", \"label\": \"Typ A\" }, { \"value\": \"b\", \"label\": \"Typ B\" }] },\n        { \"name\": \"from\", \"in\": \"query\", \"label\": \"Från datum\", \"type\": \"date\" },\n        { \"name\": \"per_page\", \"in\": \"query\", \"type\": \"number\", \"default\": 500, \"hidden\": true }\n      ],\n      \"pagination\": { \"type\": \"next-url\", \"path\": \"next_url\", \"maxPages\": 50 },\n      \"response\": { \"format\": \"json\", \"path\": \"values\", \"rename\": { \"value\": \"varde\" } },\n      \"dataset\": { \"name\": \"My service {kind}\", \"keys\": [\"id\"] }\n    }\n  ],\n  \"i18n\": {\n    \"en\": { \"name\": \"My service\", \"endpoints\": { \"list\": { \"name\": \"List records\", \"params\": { \"kind\": { \"label\": \"Kind\", \"options\": { \"a\": \"Kind A\", \"b\": \"Kind B\" } }, \"from\": { \"label\": \"From date\" } } } } }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### Top-level fields\n\n| Field | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `format` | Always `dataspatium-api/1` |\n| `id` | Unique id, lower-case letters, digits, hyphens |\n| `name`, `publisher`, `description`, `docs`, `license`, `notes` | Shown in the app |\n| `level` | 1 = no key, 2 = key required |\n| `country` | Country or area for the country filter: `SE`, `DK`, `NO`, `FI`, `IS`, `FO`, `GL`, `AX`, `NORDIC`, `EU` or `INT` |\n| `i18n` | Translations per language (Swedish is the file's base language): `{ \"en\": { \"name\", \"publisher\", \"description\", \"notes\", \"auth\": { \"help\" }, \"endpoints\": { \"<id>\": { \"name\", \"description\", \"params\": { \"<name>\": { \"label\", \"help\", \"example\", \"options\": { \"<value>\": \"label\" } } } } }, \"dataset\": { \"<id>\": \"name template\" } } }`. The app shows the texts of its language; the export contains all of them |\n| `baseUrl` | Address that the endpoints' paths are appended to |\n| `headers` | Default headers for all requests |\n| `auth` | `none`, `apiKey` (`in`: `header` or `query`, `name`, optional `prefix`), `basic`, `bearer`, `oauth2_client_credentials` (`tokenUrl`, optional `scope`); `help` and `signupUrl` are shown by the key field |\n| `rateLimit` | `requests` per `perSeconds`; requests are queued so that the limit holds, and 429 responses are waited out |\n| `family` | Special browser: `{ \"type\": \"pxweb\", \"version\": 1 or 2, \"lang\", \"maxCells\", \"urls\", \"langs\" }`, `{ \"type\": \"statbank\" }` (Statistics Denmark), `{ \"type\": \"fhi\" }` (Norwegian Institute of Public Health) or `{ \"type\": \"socialstyrelsen\" }`. `urls` gives one root per app language for PxWeb v1 (`{ \"sv\": \"...\", \"en\": \"...\" }`); `langs` states which app languages the service's `lang` parameter understands, as a list or as a map from the app's code to the service's (`{ \"nb\": \"no\" }`) |\n| `endpoints` | List of requests (see below) |\n\n### Endpoint\n\n| Field | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `id`, `name`, `description` | Identity and text in the form |\n| `method` | `GET` or `POST` |\n| `path` | Path after `baseUrl`; `{name}` is replaced by parameter values. Empty optional placeholders are removed |\n| `headers` | Extra headers |\n| `params` | Parameters, see below |\n| `body`, `bodyType` | Body for POST: a JSON template where `\"{name}\"` is replaced by the value (`json`, default), `form` or `text` |\n| `pagination` | `none`, `page` (`param`, `start`, `sizeParam`, `size`, `totalPagesPath`), `offset` (`param`, `sizeParam`, `size`, `totalPath`), `next-url` (`path` to the next address in the response), `cursor` (`path`, `param`), `link-header` (`rel`), `link-rel` (`path` to a list of links, `rel`); `maxPages` limits the number of requests |\n| `response` | `format`: `json` (`path` to the records, `explode` for a nested list), `jsonstat`, `sdmx` (SDMX-JSON 1.0 and 2.0), `csv` (`options.delimiter`, `options.skipUntil`, `options.decimal`), `geojson` (`options.geometry`), `xml` (`recordTag`), `entryscape`, `text`, `graphql` (the first list under `data` becomes the table; errors in the response are reported), `sparql` (the query variables become columns), `influx-csv` (annotated CSV from InfluxDB). `lift` lifts the fields of a nested object into columns (CouchDB `doc`, Elasticsearch `_source`, Airtable `fields`), and a `path` that is a key with dots (`@odata.nextLink`) is looked up whole. Common: `constants` (column: path in the root document), `convert` (`epoch-ms`, `epoch-s`, `number`), `rename`, `drop` |\n| `dataset` | `name` with placeholders, `keys` for the duplicate check when appending rows |\n\n### Parameter\n\n| Field | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `name` | Name in path, query or header |\n| `in` | `path`, `query`, `header`, `body`, `template` (used only by other parameters' templates), `option` (sent to the parser, e.g. geometry on or off) |\n| `type` | `string`, `number`, `boolean`, `date`, `enum` (`options`), `multi` (several values, `join` or `style: \"repeat\"`), `text`, `querylines` (lines `name=value` that become query parameters) |\n| `label`, `help`, `example`, `required`, `default`, `hidden` | Form and default values |\n| `raw` | Do not encode the value in the path (for comma-separated lists) |\n| `template` | The value is built from other parameters, for example `\"title:\\\"{q}\\\"\"` |\n| `optionsFrom` | Fetch the options from another endpoint: `{ \"endpoint\": \"stations\", \"value\": \"column\", \"label\": \"column\", \"params\": { \"parameter\": \"{parameter}\" } }` |\n\n### The table that is created\n\nJSON records are flattened (nested objects become columns with a dot in the name, lists become JSON text). JSON-stat\ngives one row per cell with a code and label column per dimension, a time column and one value column per measure.\nSDMX-JSON gives one row per observation: for each dimension a code column (`<name>_kod`) and a text column when the\ncodes have texts, the time period, the value (`varde`) and the series and observation attributes that have values.\nColumn names are cleaned as at file import (å, ä, ö become a, a, o) and types are derived with a decimal point; codes\nwith leading zeros remain text.\n\n## Writing your own configuration\n\n1. Read the service's documentation and try a request in the browser or with `curl`.\n2. Check CORS: `curl -s -D - -o /dev/null -H \"Origin: null\" \"https://api.example.org/...\"` should show\n   `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or `null`). Without that line the service works only through a proxy.\n3. Start from a preset in `presets/api/`, change it and save it as `my-service.api.json`.\n4. Add the file in Import. Errors in the configuration are shown with field names; the format is checked against a\n   schema (`src/api/schema.js`).\n5. Share the file. It never contains keys.\n\n## Limitations\n\n- MET Norway's terms require calling programs to identify themselves with a User-Agent. Browsers send their own\n  identification, which the service accepts today; keep the number of requests low and expect that the service may\n  throttle anonymous requests.\n- SDMX is read as SDMX-JSON 1.0 or 2.0 (data messages; the ECB and the OECD send it with the parameter\n  `format=jsondata` or an `Accept` header). SDMX-ML and SDMX-CSV are not parsed. JSON-stat 1.x (Statistics Denmark)\n  and 2.0 are both read.\n- PX files are not parsed in JavaScript; fetch JSON-stat or use the package pxR in R.\n- Large responses (hundreds of thousands of rows) take time to parse in the browser. Limit the selection or use\n  `maxPages`.\n- Requests are made only when you press Fetch or open a field with options from the service.\n");
Dataspatium.assets.register("docs/en/kallor.md","text","# Sources: databases, the bridge and linked files\n\nBesides files, pasted text, web addresses and the preset open APIs (see\n[api-konfiguration.md](api-konfiguration.md)), Dataspatium can fetch data from databases in three ways:\n**Access files** are read directly in the browser, **databases on your own computer or network** are reached\nthrough a small local bridge, and **database services over HTTP** (PostgREST, OData, GraphQL and others) become\nordinary sources in Import. Files chosen with the computer's file picker can also be **linked**, so that the\ndataset is read again from disk with one click. Everything happens in the browser; nothing is sent anywhere but\nto the database you name yourself.\n\n## Access files (mdb, accdb)\n\nDrop the file on the surface in Import like any other file. It is read by mdb-reader directly in the browser,\nwithout Access on the computer: the tables are listed in the *Settings* step, you choose one and get the columns\nwith the types Access declares (text, numbers, dates, yes/no). Queries, linked tables and password-protected\nfiles cannot be read this way; use the bridge below instead. dBase files (.dbf) are read by the spreadsheet importer.\n\n## The database bridge\n\nThe bridge is a small script you start on the computer when you need it, in the spirit of a local web server. It\nlistens on your own computer only (127.0.0.1), requires a token shown in its window and is read-only unless you\nstart it with writing allowed. The app fetches tables through the bridge; nothing leaves the computer.\n\n### Starting the bridge\n\n- **Windows**: open PowerShell in the app's folder and run `powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File tools\\db-bridge.ps1`.\n  The bridge speaks ODBC, OLE DB and SqlClient and thereby reaches Access (mdb and accdb through Microsoft ACE),\n  Excel, SQL Server (LocalDB and Express included), Oracle, PostgreSQL, MySQL/MariaDB, Paradox, dBase and every\n  ODBC data source (DSN) on the computer. No administrator rights are needed.\n- **macOS and Linux** (and Windows without PowerShell): `python tools/db_bridge.py` (Python 3, standard library).\n  SQLite files always work; with `pyodbc` installed (`pip install pyodbc`) ODBC sources too.\n\nThe window shows the address (`http://127.0.0.1:<port>/`) and the token. Options: `-Port` or `--port` picks the\nport, `-AllowWrite` or `--allow-write` allows changes, `-MaxRows` or `--max-rows` caps the rows per fetch\n(default 100,000) and `-AllowOrigins` or `--allow-origin` names the app addresses allowed to call the bridge. A\npage opened from the folder has the origin `null`, which is allowed, as are `localhost` and the app's address on\nGitHub Pages. Close the window, or press Ctrl+C, to stop.\n\n### Using the bridge in the app\n\n1. Choose *Database through the bridge* under *Databases* in Import, paste the address and the token and press\n   **Connect**. The token lasts for the session; *Save encrypted* stores it with the same passphrase as the API keys.\n2. Describe the source: an Access or Excel file (path on the computer), SQL Server (server, database and user, or\n   Windows sign-in), an ODBC data source (choose among the computer's DSNs) or a connection string. Press\n   **Fetch tables**.\n3. Choose a table, see the first hundred rows and press **Import**. The column types come from the database.\n4. **Own SQL** runs a query in the database's own dialect and imports the result. SELECT shows directly; other\n   statements need a bridge started with writing allowed and are approved one at a time in a dialog that shows\n   the statement.\n\nImported datasets remember their source: *Update* in Manage, or *Saved fetches* in Import, fetches the table or\nquery again and replays the recipe. Passwords are never stored with the dataset; after a restart the app asks you\nto open the source again before updating. The SQL tab in Analyse can run directly against the source last opened\nin the bridge (*Run against*).\n\n### Security\n\n- The bridge answers on 127.0.0.1 only, requires the token in every call and allows CORS only for the app's own addresses.\n- Read-only by default: only SELECT passes. With writing allowed, your approval is still needed per statement;\n  the bridge answers 428 until the app sends the approval.\n- The bridge logs the latest calls (`GET /log`) and stops when its window is closed.\n- Tokens and passwords are kept in the session or encrypted with a passphrase, never in clear text and never in\n  shared configurations.\n\n### Troubleshooting\n\n- *Provider cannot be found*: install the Microsoft Access Database Engine (ACE) in its 64-bit version, or use an\n  ODBC DSN with a 64-bit driver. The bridge is 64-bit when PowerShell is.\n- *Wrong or missing token*: copy the token from the bridge's window again; it changes at every start unless you\n  pass `-Token`.\n- *The bridge does not answer*: check that the window is open and the address is right; try another port.\n- Execution policy: the script is started with `-ExecutionPolicy Bypass`, like the app's test tools, so no\n  settings need changing.\n- An IT department can review the script (`tools/db-bridge.ps1`, about 200 lines) and start it with a fixed port and token.\n\n## Database sources over HTTP\n\nThe button **Add a database source** under *Databases* in Import makes an ordinary API source out of an address\nand a key. The app asks the service for its tables (schema discovery) and adds one endpoint per table plus a\ngeneric one where you name the table yourself. The source then works like the preset APIs: parameters, preview,\npages followed automatically and saved fetches updated with one click.\n\n| Family | Authentication | Discovery | Fetching | CORS |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| PostgREST / Supabase | `apikey` header (optional) | OpenAPI root | `select`, `order`, filters per line; pages with `limit`/`offset` | Allowed by default |\n| OData v4 | Basic (optional) | Service document | `$select`, `$filter`, `$orderby`, `$top`; `@odata.nextLink` followed | Depends on the service |\n| GraphQL | Bearer (optional) | None | You write the query; the first list under `data` becomes the table | Depends on the server |\n| CouchDB | Basic (optional) | `_all_dbs` | `_all_docs` with the documents; `skip`/`limit` | Enable in CouchDB |\n| ClickHouse | Basic (optional) | `SHOW TABLES` | SQL per table with `FORMAT JSON`; `add_http_cors_header=1` sent | Through the setting |\n| Elasticsearch / OpenSearch | API key (optional) | `_cat/indices` | `_search` with `q`; `from`/`size`, at most 10,000 hits | `http.cors.*` |\n| SPARQL | None | None | The query as a parameter; the variables become columns | Public endpoints allow it |\n| InfluxDB v2 | Token | Buckets | Flux query; annotated CSV | Check the server |\n| Google Sheets | None | None | A published or link-shared sheet as CSV | Google |\n| Airtable | Personal token | Metadata API | Records with `offset`; the fields become columns | Allowed |\n\nCORS means that the database's server must allow calls from the browser. A page opened from the folder sends the\norigin `null`; in server mode the app's address. How to enable it is in each service's documentation (link in\nthe dialog). When the server cannot be changed, the proxy setting (see [api-konfiguration.md](api-konfiguration.md))\nor the bridge through ODBC remain.\n\nKeys and passwords stay in the session or are saved encrypted; the configuration that is created can be exported\nand shared without them. The response formats `graphql`, `sparql` and `influx-csv` and `lift` (lifting a nested\nobject into columns) are described in [api-konfiguration.md](api-konfiguration.md) and can be used in your own\nconfigurations.\n\n## Linked files\n\nIn Chrome and Edge, Import has the button **Link a file on this computer**. The file is chosen with the computer's\nfile picker and imported as usual, but the dataset keeps a link to the file: **Update from disk** in Manage reads\nthe file again with the same settings, replaces the rows and replays the recipe (cleaning, new columns and joins\nare redone). The browser may ask for permission to read the file again after a restart. Firefox lacks the\nfeature; there you import the file anew. Formats read through R (SPSS, Stata, SAS, PX, RDS, Parquet) cannot be linked.\n\n## WebSocket\n\nThe app has a connection method for WebSocket (consent per purpose, JSON frames, requests and replies paired by\nid). It is used by coming sources such as Neo4j (Bolt over WebSocket, planned for 2.9) and streaming APIs; no\npresets use it yet.\n\n## Limitations\n\n- The bridge runs on the computer where the app is used; remote databases are reached through drivers or DSNs on that computer.\n- At most 100,000 rows per fetch through the bridge unless `-MaxRows` is raised; large tables are best narrowed with SQL.\n- Access files in the browser: no queries, linked tables or passwords; very large files take memory.\n- Elasticsearch gives at most 10,000 hits per search (`from` + `size`).\n- All HTTP sources require CORS; it cannot be bypassed from the browser.\n");
Dataspatium.assets.register("docs/en/r-guide.md","text","# R in Dataspatium\n\n*Analyse* mode contains an R console. R runs in the browser with WebR (R 4.5), entirely without installation.\nEverything you need is in the app folder: R, a selection of packages and Dataspatium's helper functions.\n\n## Getting started\n\n1. Open *Analyse*. R starts automatically; it takes a couple of seconds the first time.\n2. Write code in the editor and press Ctrl+Enter (Cmd+Enter on a Mac). The whole script runs; if text is selected,\n   only the selection runs. Output, messages, errors and charts are shown below the editor.\n3. Save scripts with *Save script*. Saved scripts are in the panel on the left and come along in backups.\n\n## Dataspatium's functions\n\n| Function | Does |\n|---|---|\n| `dm_list()` | Shows the datasets in the session: name, short name, folder, rows, columns, changed, id. |\n| `dm_read(\"shortname\")` | Reads a dataset as a `data.frame` with the right types: dates become `Date`, date and time `POSIXct`, yes/no `logical`, integers `integer`. The argument is preferably the short name (for example `dm_read(\"befolkning_2024\")`, which is what the button *Insert dm_read()* writes) but can also be the id, the exact name, the name regardless of case or an unambiguous prefix; `dm_list()` shows the short names. If the dataset is in a folder, `dm_read(\"Folder/Name\")` and `dm_read(\"Folder/shortname\")` work too. |\n| `dm_read(\"shortname\", columns = c(\"a\", \"b\"), where = \"a > 10\", n = 1000)` | Reads only some columns, rows meeting an SQL condition, or a limited number of rows. |\n| `attr(df$column, \"label\")`, `\"description\"`, `\"unit\"` | The column's label, description and unit from the app come along as attributes (when set); `dm_write()` takes them back, or through the arguments `labels`, `descriptions` and `units`. |\n| `dm_value_labels(x, c(\"Man\" = 1, \"Woman\" = 2), to = \"labels\")` | Value labels. A column with codes and labels becomes `haven::labelled` in `dm_read()` (a column that already holds the labels as text keeps the codes in the attribute `value_labels`), and `dm_write()` keeps them. `dm_value_labels()` sets labels (`to = NULL`), swaps codes for labels (`\"labels\"`) or back (`\"codes\"`); without labels they are removed. |\n| `dm_write(df, \"New name\", description = \"…\")` | Sends a `data.frame` to the app as a new dataset. It appears in the list when the command has finished. |\n| `theme_dataspatium()` | ggplot2 theme with the app's look. Set automatically when `library(ggplot2)` runs. |\n| `dm_palette()` | The app's colour palette (Okabe-Ito, colour-blind safe). `scale_colour_dataspatium()` and `scale_fill_dataspatium()` exist too. |\n\nExample (the bundled example data, whose columns have Swedish names):\n\n```r\ndf <- dm_read(\"befolkning_2024\")\nlibrary(dplyr)\nper_lan <- df |>\n  group_by(lan) |>\n  summarise(folkmangd = sum(folkmangd), kommuner = n()) |>\n  arrange(desc(folkmangd))\nper_lan\ndm_write(per_lan, \"Population per county 2024\")\n\nlibrary(ggplot2)\nggplot(per_lan, aes(x = reorder(lan, folkmangd), y = folkmangd / 1000)) +\n  geom_col(fill = dm_palette()[1]) +\n  coord_flip() +\n  labs(title = \"Population per county 2024\", x = NULL, y = \"Thousands\")\n```\n\n## Charts\n\nCharts are drawn to a canvas and shown in the console. Below each chart there are buttons for export to SVG (vector,\ngood for Word and the web), PNG and PDF. The export uses the same code that drew the chart, so ggplot2 charts are\nsharp in every format. The default size is 7 × 5 inches; PNG is exported at 150 dpi.\n\n## Packages\n\nThe following packages ship in the app folder and can be loaded with `library()` without internet: ggplot2, dplyr,\ntidyr, readr, stringr, lubridate, forcats, data.table, haven (SPSS, Stata, SAS), readxl, writexl, nanoparquet\n(Parquet), svglite, jsonlite, RSQLite, DBI and knitr, plus their dependencies (among them tibble, purrr, rlang, cli,\nstringi). `library(package)` installs the package from the built-in repository the first time it is used.\n\nOther packages are fetched when needed from webR's package repository (repo.r-wasm.org, more than 20,000 CRAN\npackages built for the browser): when the code calls `library()`, `require()` or `package::` for a package outside\nthe selection, it is fetched with its dependencies before the code runs. The first time, the app asks for permission\nto contact the internet for components; the packages are then stored in the browser, remain at the next start and are\nshown under *Manage → Workspace → Components*, where they can also be removed. Without a network, or if you say no,\nthe code gets the usual error that the package is missing. Packages that need system libraries not available in the\nbrowser may be missing from the repository.\n\n## R steps in recipes\n\nProcess has the action **R step** (the Code group): a function `function(df) { ... }` runs on the whole table and\nwhat it returns becomes the new table, with types from the result. The step is logged in the recipe with its code,\ncan be undone, replayed on another dataset and exported as R code (`df <- (function(df) {...})(df)`). *Preview* runs\nthe function on the first 200 rows. Packages the code uses are fetched as in the console. R charts in Visualise now\nalso export from Export, in batches and in document templates without the R tab being open: the app runs the script\nin the R session when needed.\n\n## How data moves between the app and R\n\nThe datasets are SQLite files. When you run code, the app copies the datasets that the code refers to with\n`dm_read()` into R's virtual file system under `/data`, together with an index. `dm_read()` reads the file with\nRSQLite and restores the column types from the table `_columns`. `dm_write()` writes a new SQLite file in\n`/data/out` that the app picks up and adds to the workspace when the command has finished. If you want to use\nRSQLite directly, that works: `DBI::dbConnect(RSQLite::SQLite(), \"/data/<id>.sqlite\")` where the id is shown by\n`dm_list()`.\n\nDatasets larger than 50 MB are mirrored only when they are referred to in the code.\n\n## Language\n\nMessages from `dm_list()`, `dm_read()` and `dm_write()` follow the app's language (Swedish, Danish, Norwegian,\nFinnish, Icelandic or English); the column names in `dm_list()`, however, are always the same (`namn`, `kortnamn`, `mapp`,\n`rader`, `kolumner`, `ändrad`, `id`) so that scripts work regardless of language.\n\n## Limitations\n\n- When the app is opened from a folder (file://), R code cannot be stopped while it runs. If something hangs:\n  *Restart R*. Saved scripts are not affected, but objects in the R session disappear. When the app runs from a web\n  server (localhost or https), the button *Stop* stops the code and keeps the session (see [faq.md](faq.md)).\n- Memory is limited by the browser and WebAssembly; keep datasets in R below a few hundred megabytes.\n- Input during a run (`readline()`, `menu()`) does not work.\n- Graphics windows, system commands, network calls and files outside the virtual file system are not available.\n- Help pages are included: `?dplyr::mutate` prints the help text in the console.\n");
Dataspatium.assets.register("docs/en/python.md","text","# Python in Dataspatium\n\nThe *Analyse* mode has a Python console next to the R console. Python runs in the browser with Pyodide (Python 3.14),\nwith nothing to install. Unlike R, Python does not ship in the app folder: the runtime and the packages are fetched\nfrom jsDelivr the first time you use them, after you allow it, and are then kept in the browser as components.\n\n## Getting started\n\n1. Open *Analyse* and the *Python* tab. Press *Start Python* or *Run*. The first time, the app asks for permission to\n   fetch components and downloads Python with numpy and pandas (about 22 MB); that takes half a minute on an ordinary\n   connection. The next time Python starts in a couple of seconds without the internet.\n2. Write code in the editor and press Ctrl+Enter (Cmd+Enter on a Mac). The whole script runs; if text is selected, only\n   the selection runs. Output, errors and charts appear below the editor, and the value of the last line is printed\n   as in a Python console.\n3. Save scripts with *Save script*. Saved scripts are listed in the panel on the left and travel with backups.\n\n## Dataspatium's functions\n\nThe functions have the same names as in R and are available directly in the session (`from dataspatium import *` runs\nat start).\n\n| Function | Does |\n|---|---|\n| `dm_list()` | Shows the datasets in the session as a `DataFrame` with the columns namn, kortnamn, mapp, rader, kolumner, ändrad, id. |\n| `dm_read(\"shortname\")` | Reads a dataset as a pandas `DataFrame` with the right types: dates and date-times become `datetime64`, yes/no `boolean`, integers `int64` (or `Int64` when values are missing), numbers `float64`, text `str`. The argument is preferably the short name (what the *Insert dm_read()* button writes) but can also be the id, the exact name, the name regardless of case or a unique prefix. |\n| `dm_read(\"Name\", columns=[\"a\", \"b\"], where=\"a > 10\", n=1000)` | Reads only certain columns, rows that meet an SQL condition, or a limited number of rows. |\n| `df.attrs[\"dataspatium\"]` | The columns' labels, descriptions, units, types and value labels from the app (`labels`, `descriptions`, `units`, `types`, `value_labels`). `dm_write()` takes them back, or through the arguments `labels`, `descriptions` and `units`. |\n| `dm_value_labels(df, \"sex\", {1: \"Man\", 2: \"Woman\"}, to=\"labels\")` | Value labels for one column, as a dict from code to label. `to=\"labels\"` replaces codes with labels, `to=\"codes\"` goes back, `to=None` only sets the labels, and `labels=None` removes them. The table is changed in place and `dm_write()` keeps the labels. |\n| `dm_write(df, \"New name\", description=\"…\")` | Sends a `DataFrame` to the app as a new dataset. It appears in the list when the command has finished. Without a name, the variable's name is used. |\n| `theme_dataspatium()` | matplotlib style with the app's look and palette. Applied automatically the first time matplotlib is used in the session. |\n| `dm_palette()` | The app's colour palette (Okabe-Ito, colour-blind safe) as a list; `dm_palette(n)` gives n colours. |\n\nExample:\n\n```python\ndf = dm_read(\"population_2024\")\nper_county = (df.groupby(\"county\", as_index=False)\n    .agg(population=(\"population\", \"sum\"), municipalities=(\"municipality\", \"count\"))\n    .sort_values(\"population\", ascending=False))\nper_county\ndm_write(per_county, \"Population per county 2024\")\n\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfig, ax = plt.subplots()\nax.barh(per_county[\"county\"][::-1], per_county[\"population\"][::-1] / 1000, color=dm_palette()[0])\nax.set_title(\"Population per county 2024\")\nax.set_xlabel(\"Thousands\")\n```\n\n## Charts\n\nmatplotlib draws without a screen (Agg) and every open figure is shown in the console as SVG when the command has\nfinished; the figures are then closed, so a new command starts with a clean slate. Below every chart there are\nbuttons for export to SVG (vector, good for Word and the web), PNG and PDF, at 7 × 5 inches and 150 dpi for PNG. In\nthe notebook, charts are saved as PNG.\n\n## Packages\n\nnumpy, pandas and micropip are fetched at start. Pyodide has packages built for the browser, more than 350 of\nthem: matplotlib, scipy, statsmodels, scikit-learn, networkx, shapely, geopandas, sympy, pillow, lxml, openpyxl, xlrd,\nsqlalchemy, pyarrow, duckdb, bokeh, altair and others. When the code imports a package that is not in the session, the\napp fetches the wheel with its dependencies before the code runs, checks it against the digest in Pyodide's package\nlist and keeps it in the browser; it is listed under *Manage → Workspace → Components*, where components can also be\nremoved. Pure Python packages from PyPI that are not part of Pyodide are installed with\n`import micropip; await micropip.install(\"package\")`; the app asks for permission before Python reaches the internet.\n\nDigests: the runtime files are pinned to Pyodide 314.0.7 with SHA-256 in the app; the packages are checked against\n`pyodide-lock.json`, which is in turn pinned by its digest. A file with the wrong digest is not used.\n\n## Code steps in recipes\n\nProcess has the action **Python step** (the Code group in the palette): a function `def step(df): ... return df` runs\non the whole table with pandas, and what the function returns becomes the new table, with types from the result.\nThe step is logged in the recipe with its code, can be undone, replayed on another dataset and travels with the\nexported recipe (as comments in the SQL and R code). The *Preview* button runs the function on the first 200 rows\nand shows the result's columns and a few rows before you apply. Import what you need in the code; `pd` and `np` are\nalready there. The matching **R step** takes a function `function(df) { ... }` (see [r-guide.md](r-guide.md)).\n\n## seaborn and plotly\n\nseaborn and plotly are not part of Pyodide's distribution; the app pins their wheels from PyPI with digests and\nfetches them as components when the code imports them (`import seaborn as sns`, `import plotly.express as px`).\nseaborn draws through matplotlib and is shown like other figures. plotly figures are interactive: `fig.show()`, or\n`fig` as the last line, hands the figure to the app, which draws it with plotly.js (fetched as a component the first\ntime, 4.8 MB) and the app's theme, and exports it to SVG, PNG and PDF. In the notebook, plotly figures are saved as\nPNG, with the interactive view kept as long as plotly.js is available.\n\n## Python charts in Visualise\n\nIn Visualise a chart can be a **Python script**: the dataset is `df`, and the latest chart (matplotlib or plotly) is\nshown and saved with the script. Python charts export to SVG, PNG, PDF and PowerPoint, run in batches per group\n(the script runs once per value with `df` filtered) and can be placed in document templates with\n`{{diagram \"Name\"}}`; the same now goes for R charts. In dashboards and published HTML a saved plotly chart is\ninteractive, other Python and R charts are shown as their saved image.\n\n## How data moves between the app and Python\n\nDatasets are SQLite files. When you run code, the app copies the datasets the code refers to with `dm_read()` into\nPython's virtual file system under `/data`, together with an index. `dm_read()` reads the file with the `sqlite3`\nmodule and `pandas.read_sql_query` and restores the column types from the `_columns` table. `dm_write()` writes a new\nSQLite file in `/data/out` which the app picks up and adds to the workspace when the command has finished. You can\nuse `sqlite3` directly: `sqlite3.connect(\"/data/<id>.sqlite\")` where the id is shown by `dm_list()`.\n\nDatasets larger than 50 MB are only mirrored when the code refers to them. Data moves between R and Python through\ndatasets: `dm_write()` in one language, `dm_read()` in the other.\n\n## Language\n\nMessages from `dm_list()`, `dm_read()` and `dm_write()` follow the app's language (Swedish, Danish, Norwegian,\nFinnish, Icelandic or English); the column names in `dm_list()` are always the same, so that scripts work regardless\nof language.\n\n## Limitations\n\n- When the app is opened from a folder (file://), Python code cannot be interrupted while it runs. If something hangs:\n  *Restart Python*. Saved scripts are not affected, but objects in the session are lost. When the app runs from a web\n  server (localhost or https) there is a *Stop* button that stops the code and keeps the session (see [faq.md](faq.md)).\n- Memory is limited by the browser and WebAssembly; keep datasets below a few hundred megabytes in Python.\n- Input while running (`input()`) does not work, nor do threads, processes or files outside the virtual file system.\n  Network calls from Python (for example `micropip` and `pyodide.http`) require that you have allowed components.\n- There are no interactive windows; matplotlib draws to SVG and PNG. plotly and seaborn come in a later version.\n");
Dataspatium.assets.register("docs/en/dokumentmallar.md","text","# Documents and document templates\n\nUnder **Export › Documents** you create documents from a dataset: reports, codebooks and summaries per group. A\ntemplate is plain text in Markdown with placeholders. The same template gives HTML, Word (DOCX), PDF, Markdown and\nLaTeX, and can be run in batch so that each municipality, year or other group gets its own document.\n\nThe placeholders and table keywords are Swedish words (`{{datamangd}}`, `kolumner`, `sortera`, …). They are part\nof the template language and are the same whatever language the app is shown in; the built-in templates' texts\nfollow the app's language.\n\n## How to do it\n\n1. Choose a dataset in the object panel and open the **Documents** tab in Export.\n2. Choose a template: **Report on a dataset**, **Codebook** or **Summary per group**, or a saved template of your own.\n   The template is shown on the left and the preview on the right updates as you type.\n3. Choose a format and press **Create document**. The file is downloaded.\n4. **Batch per group** creates one document per value in a column and packs them into a ZIP file with a manifest.\n5. **Save template** saves your changes in the workspace (built-in templates are saved as copies). **Export template\n   (file)** gives a `*.doc.json` that a colleague can add with **Import template (file)**. Your own templates come\n   along in the workspace backup.\n\n## The template language\n\nThe template is Markdown: `#`, `##`, `###` for headings, empty lines between paragraphs, `-` for bullet lists, `1.` for\nnumbered lists, `**bold**`, `*italic*`, `` `code` ``, `[text](https://…)` for links, `---` for a line and tables\nwith pipes.\n\nPlaceholders are written with double curly braces (Mustache). Numbers get the format of the app's language (in\nEnglish 1,234.5; in Swedish 1 234,5).\n\n| Placeholder | Content |\n|---|---|\n| `{{datamangd}}`, `{{beskrivning}}`, `{{kalla}}` | The dataset's name, description and source |\n| `{{antal_rader}}`, `{{antal_kolumner}}` | Number of rows (after filters) and columns |\n| `{{datum}}`, `{{tid}}`, `{{app}}` | Today's date, the time and the app version |\n| `{{summor.kolumn}}`, `{{medel.kolumn}}`, `{{minsta.kolumn}}`, `{{storsta.kolumn}}` | Sum, mean, minimum and maximum of a number column (replace `kolumn` with the column name) |\n| `{{grupp}}`, `{{gruppkolumn}}` | The group's value and column name in batch runs |\n| `{{#rader}} … {{/rader}}` | Repeated for each row (at most 1,000); inside, the column names are used, for example `{{kommun}}` |\n| `{{#kolumner}} … {{/kolumner}}` | Repeated for each column with `{{namn}}`, `{{typ}}`, `{{etikett}}`, `{{beskrivning}}`, `{{enhet}}` and `{{vardeetiketter}}` (for example `1 = Man; 2 = Woman`; the last three are empty unless set under Process) |\n| `{{#beskrivning}} … {{/beskrivning}}` | Shown only if the value exists (Mustache section) |\n\nColumn names are written as in the table (cleaned names, for example `folkmangd`).\n\n### Tables from the dataset\n\n```\n{{#tabell}}kolumner: kommun, folkmangd; sortera: folkmangd fallande; topp: 10; filter: lan = Skåne län; rubrik: The largest municipalities{{/tabell}}\n```\n\nAll parts are optional: without `kolumner` all columns are included, without `topp` 50 rows are shown (at most\n5,000), `sortera` takes `stigande` (ascending) or `fallande` (descending), `filter` takes conditions with `=`, `!=`,\n`>`, `>=`, `<`, `<=` or `innehåller` (contains) that can be combined with `och` or `and`, and `rubrik` is the table's\ncaption. `kalla: kolumner` instead gives a table of the dataset's columns (name, type, label, description and unit, and value labels when a column has them).\nNumber columns are right-aligned.\n\n### Charts\n\n```\n{{diagram \"Population per county\" | bredd: 600}}\n```\n\nInserts a saved chart from Visualise (Vega-Lite or D3) as an image, rendered with the same filters as the document;\n`bredd` is the width in pixels. In batch runs the chart is filtered on the group. R and Python charts are drawn by their engines when the document is created (a few seconds per chart).\n\n### Page break and codebook\n\n`{{sidbrytning}}` breaks the page in Word, PDF and LaTeX. The **Codebook** template uses extended column details:\n`{{saknade}}`, `{{unika}}`, `{{minsta}}`, `{{storsta}}`, `{{medel}}`, `{{anmarkningar}}` and `{{ar_unik}}` per column\nand `{{dubbletter}}`; they exist when the template is marked as a codebook (a copy of the built-in template keeps\nthat).\n\n## Formats\n\n| Format | What you get |\n|---|---|\n| HTML | A standalone page with built-in style and embedded images, opens in any browser and can be printed |\n| Word (DOCX) | Headings, paragraphs, lists, tables with header row and images; font Arial; opens in Word and LibreOffice |\n| PDF | A4 with page numbers, tables through jsPDF and jspdf-autotable; font Helvetica |\n| Markdown | `.md`; with images a ZIP with `figures/` |\n| LaTeX | ZIP with `main.tex` (article, booktabs, longtable, graphicx, hyperref, babel in the app's language) and `figures/`; compiled with pdflatex, xelatex or lualatex, or uploaded to Overleaf |\n\nText is always escaped for the target format: `&`, `<` and `>` in HTML, `&`, `%`, `_` and others in LaTeX, XML in DOCX.\n\n## Limitations\n\n- Templates are rendered in the browser; very large tables in documents take time, especially in PDF and Word.\n- PDF and Word use standard fonts (Helvetica and Arial) instead of the app's Inter.\n- The Markdown support is a selection: no footnotes, no nested lists, no headings below level four.\n- LaTeX is not compiled in the browser.\n");
Dataspatium.assets.register("docs/en/dela.md","text","# Share\n\nThe Share mode gathers everything about giving data to other people and devices. Export is about files to your own\ncomputer; Share about bundles, GitHub, devices, the share menu, publishing and receiving. Before datasets leave\nthe app they go through a review of personal data (see below).\n\n## Bundle\n\nA bundle is a ZIP file with chosen datasets and what belongs to them: charts, dashboards, templates, notebooks and\nscripts, together with a message, the sender's name and a change log of what has been done with each dataset since\nit was last shared. *Create bundle* opens the dialog with all datasets preselected; with a dataset selected there is\nalso *Bundle only this one*. The recipient opens the bundle under *Receive* and sees the message and the changes\nbefore anything is taken in. The inspector shows how much there is to share. See also [format.md](format.md) on how\nthe bundle is built.\n\n## GitHub\n\nThe workspace as a folder in a GitHub repository, preferably private. Enter the repository (`owner/name`), branch\nand folder, and a fine-grained access token that may only read and write the contents of that repository. The token\nis used for the session or stored encrypted; nothing is sent until you press *Push to GitHub* or *Fetch from\nGitHub*. Only changed files are sent, in one commit with your message. When fetching you choose, as for bundles,\nwhether datasets that already exist are replaced (your own version is kept under *Versions*). The first time, the\napp asks for permission to contact GitHub (see *Internet access* in [anvandarguide.md](anvandarguide.md)).\n\n## Devices\n\nDirect transfer to phones, tablets and other computers over the local network, without a server. The computer\nshows a QR code; the phone scans it with the camera and opens Dataspatium Mobile, which receives bundles and files,\nshows the tables and can forward them or hand them to another computer. The first time, the phone shows a code\nthat is typed into the computer and the devices are paired; after that one scan is enough. Both devices show the\nsame verification code, and the receiver approves before anything is received. Two computers connect with pasted\ncodes. The whole flow, requirements and troubleshooting are in [mobil.md](mobil.md). The button *QR code for the\nmobile page* shows the address to scan for opening the mobile page the first time.\n\n## Review before sharing\n\nWhen datasets are bundled, sent to a device or shared through the share menu, the app looks through the columns\nfor identity numbers (Swedish, Danish, Norwegian, Finnish and Icelandic), e-mail addresses and phone numbers, by\ncolumn names and values. If something is found, a dialog lets you choose *Keep*, *Pseudonymise* (the same value\ngives the same code, but the value cannot be read back) or *Drop the column* for each column. Only the copy that\nis shared changes; the dataset in the app stays as it is, and the bundle's manifest records which columns were\nchanged. The review does not see everything: names, addresses and free text you have to judge yourself.\n\n## Share menu\n\nThe computer's or phone's own share menu: send a file to e-mail, Teams, messaging apps or other apps. Choose whether\nthe selected dataset is shared as a file (CSV, Excel, ODS or JSON) or whether all datasets are shared as a bundle,\nand press *Share*. The share menu for files exists in Edge and Chrome on Windows and on mobile; in other browsers\n(Firefox) the file is saved instead, and the button is then called *Save file*. On a phone with Dataspatium Mobile\ninstalled, files can also be shared *to* Dataspatium from other apps; they end up under *Received* on the mobile\npage.\n\n## Publish\n\nA standalone HTML file that works without Dataspatium and can be put on an intranet or sent: dashboards (with the\ncharts embedded) and documents from templates, filled with the selected dataset. Published files contain data and\ncharts in clear text; share them only with those who may see the information.\n\n## Receive\n\nOpen a bundle from a file (drop it on the surface) or from a web address. Sender, message and changes are shown\nfirst; datasets that already exist can be replaced with the bundle's version, otherwise they are skipped. A backup\n(ZIP from *Back up the workspace*) can also be opened here, but belongs under Manage, Workspace.\n\n## What is not possible\n\nA browser cannot be a server, so others cannot fetch data from your computer when it is off or when they are not on\nthe same network; then a file or a bundle passes through the share menu, a cloud folder (the portable folder under\nManage can live in OneDrive or Dropbox) or GitHub. On the same network the transfer goes directly between the\ndevices (the Devices tab). `mailto:` links cannot carry attachments; use the share menu or save the file and attach\nit.\n");
Dataspatium.assets.register("docs/en/mobil.md","text","# Dataspatium Mobile and transfer between devices\n\nDataspatium Mobile is a small page for phones and tablets that receives, shows and forwards data from Dataspatium.\nIt runs from the web (<https://marwiss.github.io/Dataspatium/m/> or the same folder as the app on a web server of\nyour own) and can be installed as an app. The whole of Dataspatium does not run on the phone: the mobile page is a\ncourier, a receiver, a viewer and a forwarder.\n\nThe transfer goes directly between the devices over the local network with WebRTC, without a server and without\ndata passing through the internet. The computer shows a QR code, the phone scans it, and both show the same\nverification code. The same technique connects two computers with pasted codes.\n\n## The first time: pair the phone\n\n1. On the computer: open *Share → Devices*, leave *Connect to* at *New device* and click *Show QR code*.\n2. On the phone: open the camera and point it at the QR code. The mobile page opens in the browser (the first\n   time it is fetched from the web; afterwards it sits in the phone's cache). The phone shows a 52-character code.\n3. On the computer: type the phone's code into the field below the QR code and click *Connect*. A computer with a\n   camera will be able to scan the phone's QR code instead in a coming version.\n4. Both devices now show the same six-digit verification code. If it matches: click *Pair the device* on the\n   computer. The phone is told and stores the pairing too.\n\nNext time it is enough to choose the phone under *Connect to* on the computer, show the QR code and scan: the\nconnection is made without any code back. The pairing consists of the phone's certificate and a secret the\ndevices share; it is stored in the browser on both sides and can be removed under *Paired devices* (computer) or by\nclearing site data (phone).\n\n## Sending from the computer\n\nUnder *Send* you choose the selected datasets or all datasets as a bundle, or files from the computer. Datasets\nfirst go through the review before sharing (see [dela.md](dela.md)): columns that look like identity numbers,\ne-mail or phone numbers can be pseudonymised or dropped in the copy. The phone asks before receiving and shows the\nverification code once more. The transfer goes in blocks with a checksum; if it breaks, it resumes from the blocks\nthat have already arrived when you connect again.\n\n## On the phone\n\n- **Received** lists bundles and files with the number of datasets, size and sender. A bundle opens with the list\n  of its datasets; a dataset is shown as a table with a filter and paging, with the first column fixed when the\n  table scrolls sideways.\n- **Forward** sends the bundle or file through the phone's share sheet (e-mail, messages, cloud storage).\n- **Send to a computer** queues the bundle: scan the other computer's QR code and it is sent there once the\n  connection is up. That is the courier flow computer → phone → computer, also between two networks.\n- The phone can also send its own files (*Send files from the phone*) to a connected computer; they are imported\n  there as datasets, or opened under *Receive* if they are bundles.\n- Apps on the phone can share files to Dataspatium Mobile through the share sheet when the mobile page is\n  installed as an app; the files end up under *Received*.\n- *Settings* holds the phone's name (what the computer shows), its code and the language.\n\n## Computer to computer\n\nChoose *Another computer (paste a code)* on one computer and paste the code from the other computer's *Show QR\ncode* view (the *Copy* button below the QR code). The first computer then shows an answer code that is pasted on\nthe other under *Code from the device*. From there everything works as between computer and phone, pairing\nincluded.\n\n## Requirements and limitations\n\n- The devices must reach each other on the network. On home networks and most office networks it works;\n  networks that isolate clients from each other (guest networks, some corporate networks) stop the connection.\n  Then share the computer's or phone's own network (tethering) for a while.\n- The computer needs Edge, Chrome or Firefox. The phone needs a browser with WebRTC and, for scanning, a camera;\n  in Android Chrome QR codes are read directly in the page, in other browsers the code is opened through the camera\n  app or pasted.\n- The phone needs the web the first time to fetch the mobile page; afterwards it works without a network except\n  for the transfer itself, which goes locally.\n- No data is sent to any server. The QR code holds the computer's network address and a one-time key; the\n  verification code protects against someone else on the network joining in. Pair only devices whose verification\n  code you have compared.\n- A transfer stays in memory; very large bundles (several hundred megabytes) can be too much for a phone.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n- *Waiting for the device to connect* never goes away: the devices are on different networks, or the network\n  blocks traffic between them. Try tethering from the phone and connect the computer to it.\n- *The other side is not the device the code points to*: the code that was typed belongs to another phone. Type it\n  again.\n- The verification codes differ: disconnect. Someone else may have scanned the code.\n- The log at the bottom of the computer shows every step of the connection.\n");
Dataspatium.assets.register("docs/en/faq.md","text","# Frequently asked questions\n\n## Where is my data stored?\n\nIn the browser's own database (IndexedDB) on the computer and in the browser profile you use. Nothing is sent\nanywhere. If you clear site data, switch profile or uninstall the browser, the data disappears. So take backups\nunder *Manage → Workspace*, or use a portable folder in Edge and Chrome so that the workspace also lives as\nordinary files on disk (see [kom-igang.md](kom-igang.md)).\n\n## Do I need internet?\n\nNo. Internet is needed only when you fetch data from an API, push to or fetch from GitHub, click *Check for updates*\nor use something that does not ship in the folder: some components (for example base maps) and R packages outside the\nbuilt-in selection are fetched when needed and then stored locally. The first time the app needs the internet for a\npurpose it asks for permission; the answer can be remembered and changed under *Manage → Workspace → Internet\naccess*, where the log of requests is too. Everything else, R included, works without a network.\n\n## Why does it take time the first time I use R?\n\nR is loaded as WebAssembly (about 50 MB) and starts in a few seconds. Packages such as ggplot2 are installed in\nthe R session when you use them for the first time, which takes a few more seconds. The R session starts over when\nyou reload the page, but your datasets and scripts remain.\n\n## Can I interrupt R code that is stuck?\n\nYes, when the app runs from a web server (for example `python -m http.server 8080` on your own computer, or https on\nan intranet): then the button *Stop* sits next to *Run* in the R tab, and the R session with its objects remains. When\nthe app is opened from a folder (file://) the browser does not allow the mechanism this needs; use *Restart R* in\nAnalyse instead. The datasets are not affected.\n\n## How does Python work in the app?\n\nPython (Pyodide) does not ship in the folder. The first time you press *Start Python* or *Run* in the Python tab, the\napp asks for permission to fetch components and downloads Python with numpy and pandas (about 22 MB) from jsDelivr,\nchecked against known digests. After that everything is kept in the browser and Python starts without the internet.\nPackages the code imports (matplotlib, scipy, scikit-learn and the others Pyodide offers) are fetched the same way\nwhen needed. See [python.md](python.md).\n\n## Why does an API not work?\n\nMost often the API lacks CORS support, that is, the browser is not allowed to read the response. Then Dataspatium\ncan only reach it through a proxy that you run yourself (setting under Import). Some APIs also require a key, for\nexample Boverket and Lantmäteriet Direkt. See [api-konfiguration.md](api-konfiguration.md), which also describes\nhow to get keys.\n\n## How are API keys stored?\n\nEither only in memory during the session or encrypted with a passphrase you choose. Never in clear text, and they\nnever come along when you share a configuration.\n\n## How large datasets can the app handle?\n\nA couple of million rows in Edge and Chrome, somewhat fewer in Firefox; the browser's memory sets the limit.\nMeasured times for one million rows are in [utveckling.md](../utveckling.md) (Swedish). Text files of 64 MB or more are read\nin chunks, so the import itself does not require the whole file to fit in memory as text.\n\n## What is the difference between Firefox and Edge or Chrome?\n\nLook and features are the same, but Firefox lacks folder access, so the portable folder is not available there.\nBackup as ZIP works everywhere.\n\n## Can the app run from a web server or an intranet?\n\nYes. Put the folder unchanged on any static web server; no server functions are needed. Over https, and on your own\ncomputer over `http://localhost`, the app registers a service worker (`sw-coi.js`) that makes the page *cross-origin\nisolated*, and then R code can be stopped. The first time, the page reloads once when it takes over. Over plain http on\nan intranet the browser allows no service workers, but a server that itself sends the headers\n`Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin` and `Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: credentialless` gives the same. Everything\nelse works as from the folder. The browser stores data per address, so the workspace in the folder and the one on the\nserver are separate; move between them with a backup or a bundle.\n\nFrom a web server, including <https://marwiss.github.io/Dataspatium/>, the app can be installed as an app of its own:\nin Edge and Chrome through the *Install* icon in the address bar or the browser menu. It then gets its own window and\nicon, the files are stored in the browser so that it works without a network, and storage becomes persistent. New\nversions are fetched when the app is opened again with a network.\n\n## Can I open index.html directly inside the ZIP file?\n\nNo. Unpack the ZIP file first (right-click and choose *Extract all* in Windows) and then open `index.html` in the\nunpacked folder.\n\n## How do I update?\n\nDownload the new version and unpack it into a new folder. Your data lives in the browser, not in the folder, so it\nremains when you open the new version's `index.html` on the same computer and in the same browser. Under the help\nbutton there is *Check for updates*, which compares your version with the latest release on GitHub. The check is\nmade only when you click.\n\n## Can I change the language?\n\nYes. The first time the app is opened you choose a language; afterwards the language button at the top right (the\nicon with letters) or the command *Language* in the palette opens the same chooser. Swedish, Danish, Norwegian\n(Bokmål), Finnish, Icelandic and English are available. The app reloads in the chosen language after unsaved\nchanges have been saved. The choice is stored in the browser, so each computer and profile has its own.\n\n## Are there sources from the other Nordic countries?\n\nYes. Under *Open APIs* in Import there are the statistics offices of Denmark, Norway, Finland, Iceland,\nGreenland, the Faroe Islands and Åland, Nordic Statistics, the Norwegian Institute of Public Health, the\nBrønnøysund register, the Norwegian Mapping Authority, MET Norway, THL's Sotkanet, PRH's company register,\nDigitraffic, the Finnish Meteorological Institute, the Icelandic Met Office and the Danish and Finnish data\nportals. The country buttons above the list choose which countries' sources are shown; the choice is stored in\nthe browser. All sources have been checked for CORS and need no key. See\n[api-konfiguration.md](api-konfiguration.md).\n\n## Can I have the app open in several tabs?\n\nYes. The tabs share the same workspace and keep each other informed: if you save or delete something in one tab,\nthe others reload their lists and log what happened. Still, do not edit the same dataset in two tabs at the same\ntime; if both have unsaved changes, the one that saves last wins.\n\n## Can another local HTML file read my data?\n\nIn Edge and Chrome all files opened via file:// share the same storage. So do not open unknown HTML files locally\nwhile you have sensitive data in the app. Keys are never stored in clear text. Firefox keeps local files apart.\n\n## May I use the app at work?\n\nYes, if the employer is a public authority, municipality, region, school, university or a non-profit organisation,\nthat is, non-commercial use. Commercial use requires permission from the licensor. See\n[licenser.md](licenser.md).\n\n## How do I make a map of municipalities or counties?\n\nClick *Swedish municipality boundaries (SCB)* or *Swedish county boundaries (SCB)* in Import; the boundaries ship with the app\n(SCB's simplified boundaries for thematic maps, CC0). Then join your values to the boundaries on the municipality\ncode or county code in Process (*Join*), and choose the chart type *Map* in Visualise. Points with longitude and\nlatitude (for example measuring stations) are drawn with *Point map*, with the boundaries as background. Your own\nboundaries can be imported from GeoJSON, shapefile or GeoPackage. See [visualisera.md](visualisera.md).\n\n## How do I get municipality or county from coordinates?\n\nChoose *Geodata > Municipality and county from coordinates* in Process (through Actions or the palette). The\naction looks up each point's municipality and county in SCB's bundled boundaries and adds the columns kommunkod,\nkommun, lanskod and lan; coordinates in SWEREF 99 TM are recognised. The boundaries cover the land area, so points\nin water become empty. The same group has centroid and area from geometry and conversion between SWEREF 99 TM and\nWGS 84. See [bearbeta.md](bearbeta.md).\n\n## How do I combine several datasets in one SQL query?\n\nEvery dataset has a short name (shown below the name in the object panel, changed under Manage > Rename). In the\nSQL tab under Analyse the selected dataset is called `data` but is also reached by its short name, and all other\ndatasets are reached by their short names: `select k.kommun, b.folkmangd from befolkning_2024 b join\nkommungranser k on k.kommunkod = b.kommunkod`. The result can be saved as a new dataset. In R,\n`dm_read(\"shortname\")` reads the same dataset. See [bearbeta.md](bearbeta.md).\n\n## How do I check that the data is up to standard?\n\n*Data quality* in Process profiles all columns and suggests actions. *Rules* let you write down requirements (must\nnot be missing, unique, range, list, pattern, length or an SQL condition) and run them after every import; failing\nrows can be saved as a dataset of their own and the result downloaded as a report. The rules are stored in the\ndataset and come along when you share it. See [bearbeta.md](bearbeta.md).\n\n## Can I remove personal identity numbers before I share a dataset?\n\nYes. The action *Pseudonymise* in Process replaces identifiers with salted hashes, masked values or running\nnumbers, and the data quality profile suggests it when a column looks like it contains personal identity numbers.\nIt is pseudonymisation, not anonymisation: other columns can still point out persons, so go through the whole\ndataset before you share it. See [bearbeta.md](bearbeta.md).\n\n## How do I share data with a colleague?\n\nEasiest in the *Share* mode: under *Bundle* you choose datasets and whether charts, dashboards, templates, notebooks\nand scripts should come along, write a message and send the ZIP file, for example through the *Share menu* tab\n(e-mail, Teams and other apps). The colleague opens it under *Receive*, sees your message and what has changed and can\nreplace their own versions. Comments, folders and tags come along. On the same network the bundle goes directly between the computers or through the\nphone (the *Devices* tab, see [mobil.md](mobil.md)). If you work on the same workspace, you can also use a shared\nGitHub repository (the *GitHub* tab). Before anything leaves the app, the datasets are reviewed for personal\ndata, which can be pseudonymised or dropped in the copy. Dashboards and documents can be published as standalone HTML files (the\n*Publish* tab). You can also export a single dataset as SQLite (keeps types, labels, comments and history) or take a\nbackup of the whole workspace. Charts and document templates can be exported as JSON files, API configurations as\n`.api.json`, and recipes as JSON, R or SQL. See [dela.md](dela.md).\n\n## How do I open the beginner's course again?\n\nClick the question mark at the top right and choose *Open the beginner's course*, or press Ctrl+K and type *course*.\nThe course continues where you left off; if you have finished it, it starts again from the beginning. It is\navailable in all six languages.\n\n## How do I keep order among many datasets?\n\nGive them folders and tags: *Organise* in Manage, or drag a dataset to a folder in the object panel. Folders can\nhave subfolders (`Economy/2026`), the filter field searches name, tag and folder, and the checkboxes select several\ndatasets for batch actions. Folder and tags travel with the dataset in bundles and backups, and in R `dm_list()`\nshows the folder.\n\n## What are components?\n\nParts of the app that do not ship in the folder, to keep it small: for example base maps and R packages outside the\nbuilt-in selection. They are fetched from a fixed address when something needs them, after you have given\npermission, and are checked against a known checksum where one exists. They are then stored in the browser and need\nnot be fetched again. Under *Manage → Workspace → Components* you see which ones exist and how large they are, and\ncan fetch them in advance or remove them.\n\n## Can I use the app on my phone?\n\nThe whole app is made for computers, but the phone can take part: Dataspatium Mobile\n(<https://marwiss.github.io/Dataspatium/m/>) receives bundles and files from the computer over the local network,\nshows the tables, forwards them and hands them to another computer. Open *Share → Devices* on the computer and scan\nthe QR code; see [mobil.md](mobil.md). On tablets in server mode the whole app can be used: in narrow windows the\nmode switcher shows icons and the panels open as sheets over the work area.\n\n## How do I move data to a phone without the internet?\n\nThe computer and the phone only need the same local network; the transfer goes directly between them with WebRTC\nand passes no server. The first time, the phone has to fetch the mobile page from the web (or from a web server of\nyour own on the intranet); after that it sits in the cache. If there is no shared network: turn on tethering on the\nphone and connect the computer to it. See [mobil.md](mobil.md).\n\n## Where is the help?\n\nIn the app: the **?** button at the top right or F1 opens the handbook at the chapter of the mode you are in, with\nsearch over every section and links between the documents. The same texts are in [docs/](anvandarguide.md) on\nGitHub. The beginner's course opens from the handbook or with Ctrl+K.\n\n## Can I show a dashboard on a large screen?\n\nYes. Open the dashboard in Visualise and click **Present**: the dashboard fills the window, the arrow keys go one\nchart at a time, F gives full screen and Esc ends. The shared filters stay at the top. See\n[visualisera.md](visualisera.md).\n\n## Can I read an Access database or a SQL Server table?\n\nYes. Access files (mdb, accdb) are dropped on the surface in Import and read directly in the browser. SQL Server,\nODBC sources, Excel and Access queries are reached through the database bridge, a small script you start on the\ncomputer; services such as PostgREST, OData and GraphQL are added as sources with an address. See\n[kallor.md](kallor.md).\n\n## Can't find the answer here?\n\nRead [anvandarguide.md](anvandarguide.md) or open an issue on GitHub (see\n[CONTRIBUTING.md](../../CONTRIBUTING.md)).\n");
Dataspatium.assets.register("docs/en/licenser.md","text","# Licences\n\nThis is an explanation in English. The full licence text in [LICENSE](../../LICENSE) applies. This text is not legal\nadvice.\n\n## Dataspatium's own code\n\nDataspatium's own code, documentation and presets (everything except the folder `vendor/`) are licensed under\n**PolyForm Strict 1.0.0** (<https://polyformproject.org/licenses/strict/1.0.0>). The licensor is Dataspatium's\nauthor, who can be reached through the GitHub account [marwiss](https://github.com/marwiss).\n\nWhat you may do:\n\n- Download and use the app for non-commercial purposes: privately, for study, research, education and charity, and\n  within public authorities, municipalities, regions and other public institutions, however the activity is funded.\n- Use the app on as many computers as you like within your organisation.\n- Create your own datasets, recipes, charts, R scripts, API configurations and document templates with the app and\n  share them as you like. They are your own works, not part of the app.\n\nWhat you may not do without written permission from the licensor:\n\n- Use the app in commercial activity, for example in consulting assignments or as part of a product or service.\n- Redistribute the app, in whole or in part, or publish your own copies. (GitHub's terms of service allow the\n  repository to be forked on GitHub; that gives no rights beyond it.)\n- Modify the code or build your own works on it.\n\nIf you want to use Dataspatium on other terms, open an issue in the repository or contact the licensor there.\n\n## Third-party components\n\nEverything under `vendor/` is third-party code with its own licences, which are not affected by Dataspatium's\nlicence. Each library, its version and licence are listed in\n[THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md](../../THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md), together with the licence texts.\n\n| Component | Licence | What it means |\n|---|---|---|\n| JavaScript libraries (sql.js, Dexie, D3, Vega, PapaParse, SheetJS, docx, jsPDF, fflate, CodeMirror and others) | MIT, BSD-3-Clause, Apache-2.0, ISC | Free to use and distribute as long as the licence text and notices come along |\n| The font Inter | SIL Open Font License 1.1 | May be embedded in the app; may not be sold on its own |\n| Lucide icons (also the app's mark and favicon) | ISC | As above |\n| webR (the JavaScript part) | MIT | As above |\n| R (R.wasm and others) | GPL-2 or GPL-3 | Free software. Distributed unchanged in binary form; the source code is available from the R project and the webR project in the stated versions |\n| R packages in `vendor/webr-repo` | Mostly MIT; also LGPL (DBI, RSQLite), MPL-2.0 (data.table), GPL (Rcpp, pxR, knitr, svglite and others) | Distributed unchanged; the source code is available on CRAN in the stated versions |\n| Python (Pyodide, CPython and packages such as numpy, pandas and matplotlib; fetched on demand, not in the folder) | MPL-2.0 (Pyodide, micropip), PSF-2.0 (CPython), BSD-3-Clause (numpy, pandas), the matplotlib licence (PSF-based), MIT and similar for the rest | Fetched unchanged from jsDelivr and kept in the browser; the source code is with the Pyodide project and on PyPI in the versions named in the app's catalogue (Pyodide 314.0.7) |\n| plotly.js (fetched on demand for plotly charts), seaborn and plotly (Python wheels from PyPI, fetched on demand) | MIT (plotly.js, plotly), BSD-3-Clause (seaborn) | Fetched unchanged from jsDelivr and PyPI, pinned to version and digest, and kept in the browser |\n\nDataspatium's code does not link against R. R runs as a separate interpreter in a worker and exchanges R code, SQLite\nfiles and images with the app through messages; that is the kind of co-distribution (\"mere aggregation\") the GPL\nallows, and the R parts keep all their GPL rights for you as a user. Example data in `presets/` comes from open\nsources and keeps its original licences (CC0 or CC BY according to each agency). The municipality and county\nboundaries in `presets/geo/` are SCB's *Digitala gränser* under CC0 1.0, converted to longitude and latitude. The\nDanish boundaries are DAGI 1:500 000 from Geodatastyrelsen (now Klimadatastyrelsen), free data that may be used and\nshared with attribution; the Norwegian ones come from Kartverket's N1000 Kartdata and the Finnish ones from Statistics\nFinland, both under CC BY 4.0 (attribution \"© Kartverket\" and \"Tilastokeskus / Statistics Finland\"). See\n`presets/geo/KALLA.md`. Data fetched from the open APIs is covered by each publisher's licence, which is shown\nwith the source in the app.\n");
